1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:17,884
A makeover project
for your most unhinged leftovers.

2
00:00:17,951 --> 00:00:21,421
The best chefs in Korea will
turn your fridge inside out.

3
00:00:21,488 --> 00:00:23,490
The classy cooking show has returned.

4
00:00:23,556 --> 00:00:27,193
<i>-Chef & My Fridge!</i>
<i>-My Fridge!</i>

5
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,762
-Welcome.
-It's great to see you.

6
00:00:28,828 --> 00:00:30,530
They have the same hairstyle.

7
00:00:30,597 --> 00:00:32,098
They did their hair the same way.

8
00:00:32,165 --> 00:00:33,466
He copied me.

9
00:00:33,533 --> 00:00:35,001
-Tubby Twins.
-Tubby Twins.

10
00:00:35,068 --> 00:00:36,803
They're back in their seats.

11
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:39,773
THEY ENJOYED
THE MAIN CHARACTER LIFE FOR ONE EPISODE

12
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:40,974
They've gone back.

13
00:00:41,041 --> 00:00:44,210
Nam-no, you still seem bloated

14
00:00:44,277 --> 00:00:47,113
-from the meals you had.
-But I'm not bloated.

15
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:49,716
-No?
-You cut your sideburns like MacGyver.

16
00:00:49,783 --> 00:00:51,117
-It's DEX.
-His sideburns are gone.

17
00:00:51,518 --> 00:00:53,319
WHO?

18
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:54,454
You're trying to be DEX?

19
00:00:54,521 --> 00:00:56,589
-BIGX.
-BIGX?

20
00:00:56,890 --> 00:00:58,324
VERY SELF-AWARE

21
00:00:58,391 --> 00:01:01,694
Chef Sung-jun said he has learned a lot.

22
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:03,696
Actually, it wasn't too pleasant for me.

23
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:05,231
-Why?
-Why?

24
00:01:05,298 --> 00:01:06,800
I thought I was the main character,

25
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:09,169
but he started
spitting out food and stuff.

26
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:12,105
<i>-It felt like he took the spotlight.</i>
<i>-Right.</i>

27
00:01:12,172 --> 00:01:13,807
HE EVEN ANALYZED THE RATINGS…

28
00:01:13,873 --> 00:01:15,375
I PREDICT ABOUT 3.8%!

29
00:01:15,442 --> 00:01:17,143
I'LL SHAVE MY HEAD IF THAT HAPPENS!

30
00:01:17,410 --> 00:01:21,681
THEY MADE A PLEDGE FOR THE VIEWERSHIP

31
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:23,783
FAILED MISERABLY

32
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,719
THEY GET TO KEEP THEIR HAIR…
VIEWER RATINGS REACH 2.462%

33
00:01:27,287 --> 00:01:29,756
I came to realize
that I have a lot more fun

34
00:01:29,823 --> 00:01:31,891
-when I sit here.
-Is that so?

35
00:01:31,958 --> 00:01:33,493
And now,

36
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:36,563
time to introduce today's fridge owners…

37
00:01:36,629 --> 00:01:38,832
-Who could they be?
-…our two amazing guests.

38
00:01:38,898 --> 00:01:41,668
Please welcome today's fridge owners!

39
00:01:41,734 --> 00:01:42,902
Come on out!

40
00:01:44,204 --> 00:01:45,939
I wonder who they are.

41
00:01:46,005 --> 00:01:47,707
Today's guests!

42
00:01:48,541 --> 00:01:52,445
Please welcome actors
Joo Sang-uk and Chae Jung-an!

43
00:01:52,512 --> 00:01:53,847
-Hello.
-Why, hello.

44
00:01:53,913 --> 00:01:55,248
-Hello.
-Please say hello.

45
00:01:56,116 --> 00:01:57,484
Grab your seats.

46
00:01:58,218 --> 00:01:59,986
-Hello.
-My gosh.

47
00:02:00,053 --> 00:02:02,322
We're here today with two amazing actors.

48
00:02:02,388 --> 00:02:06,259
He played the craziest villain
in <i>Agent Kim Reactivated</i>

49
00:02:06,326 --> 00:02:08,862
and rekindling his popularity once again.

50
00:02:08,928 --> 00:02:11,197
Please welcome Chairman Joo, Joo Sang-uk!

51
00:02:11,264 --> 00:02:14,100
-It's great to be here.
-Good to have you.

52
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:16,569
She's the icon
of the unforgettable ex-girlfriend,

53
00:02:16,636 --> 00:02:19,906
but she's also a fun entertainer
who's bold and down-to-earth.

54
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:22,008
-Please welcome Chae Jung-an!
-Chae Jung-an!

55
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:23,076
-Welcome.
-Hello.

56
00:02:23,309 --> 00:02:24,544
HAPPY TO MEET YOU

57
00:02:24,611 --> 00:02:26,112
Jung-an is very well known

58
00:02:26,179 --> 00:02:29,182
among men like Sung-ju, Jung-hwan,
Hyun-suk, and Poong.

59
00:02:29,249 --> 00:02:32,318
-We know all about her.
-Of course.

60
00:02:32,385 --> 00:02:34,354
-Just the four of you?
-We…

61
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:37,257
-All of us. This side.
-And me.

62
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:39,259
-And me.
-And Jong-won.

63
00:02:39,425 --> 00:02:40,827
THANK YOU

64
00:02:40,894 --> 00:02:44,497
Sang-uk, I heard
you couldn't wait to meet the chefs.

65
00:02:44,564 --> 00:02:45,832
I enjoy the show so much.

66
00:02:45,899 --> 00:02:48,268
It's not easy to cook within 15 minutes,

67
00:02:48,334 --> 00:02:51,371
and I find myself wondering
what they actually taste like.

68
00:02:52,539 --> 00:02:54,574
So thank you for having me today,

69
00:02:54,641 --> 00:02:55,909
and I look forward to it.

70
00:02:56,543 --> 00:02:59,979
Jung-an also wondered
why our show had never invited her.

71
00:03:00,046 --> 00:03:03,449
-Really?
-This is a long-running program, right?

72
00:03:03,516 --> 00:03:05,618
It's like the <i>Country Diaries</i>
of variety shows.

73
00:03:07,053 --> 00:03:09,455
The <i>Country Diaries</i> of variety shows?

74
00:03:09,522 --> 00:03:11,991
I've always been curious
and wanted to be here.

75
00:03:12,058 --> 00:03:15,628
She should've been here ages ago.
The invitation was belated.

76
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:16,629
It's okay.

77
00:03:16,696 --> 00:03:19,199
Isn't the show
in its best condition right now?

78
00:03:19,933 --> 00:03:20,767
"Condition"?

79
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:22,368
-Whose condition?
-Overall.

80
00:03:22,435 --> 00:03:23,836
-Everyone.
-People here.

81
00:03:23,903 --> 00:03:25,805
-Right now?
-We're the hottest now.

82
00:03:25,872 --> 00:03:27,173
The chefs are on fire now.

83
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:29,175
-We're doing great.
-Starting with Chef Nam-no,

84
00:03:29,242 --> 00:03:31,711
I heard everyone was in great condition.

85
00:03:31,778 --> 00:03:34,214
-The freshness of our chefs.
-Freshness.

86
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:36,082
She's so candid.

87
00:03:36,149 --> 00:03:37,750
-She's feisty.
-I know.

88
00:03:37,817 --> 00:03:41,020
I hear your friends also love the show.

89
00:03:41,087 --> 00:03:42,922
I want to say something to them.

90
00:03:42,989 --> 00:03:44,924
-To your friends?
-Yes.

91
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:47,026
Many of them have become single again.

92
00:03:48,328 --> 00:03:49,596
-Again?
-That happens.

93
00:03:49,662 --> 00:03:51,431
KINDLY CHECKING ON THE "RETURNED" SINGLES

94
00:03:51,497 --> 00:03:54,567
I'm on the show,
Bo-hyeon and Hyeon-gyeong.

95
00:03:54,634 --> 00:03:56,436
You're sharing their names?

96
00:03:56,502 --> 00:03:58,871
-Bo-hyeon and Hyeon-gyeong.
-Was that too abrupt?

97
00:03:58,938 --> 00:04:01,708
They must be very close.

98
00:04:01,774 --> 00:04:03,543
-She's still the same.
-She is.

99
00:04:03,610 --> 00:04:07,413
Jung-hwan was excited to hear
that Jung-an would be coming.

100
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:08,615
-Me?
-He seemed happy.

101
00:04:09,082 --> 00:04:11,384
WHAT'S THE STORY BETWEEN THEM?

102
00:04:11,451 --> 00:04:13,786
<i>-Was it seven or eight years ago?</i>
<i>-It's been a while.</i>

103
00:04:13,853 --> 00:04:15,154
When we shot that show,

104
00:04:15,221 --> 00:04:17,757
people from her agency
all came along to keep an eye on her,

105
00:04:17,824 --> 00:04:20,260
-to see if she doesn't say anything weird.
-Right.

106
00:04:20,326 --> 00:04:22,061
-She's too bold.
-She is.

107
00:04:22,128 --> 00:04:24,364
I get way too excited
when I'm on variety shows.

108
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:27,233
YES, WE CAN SEE THAT…

109
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:28,868
I love to joke around.

110
00:04:28,935 --> 00:04:31,904
But there is a line to draw
when I'm on TV, so I must've said

111
00:04:31,971 --> 00:04:33,273
many unnecessary things.

112
00:04:33,339 --> 00:04:34,707
I'm not like that now.

113
00:04:34,774 --> 00:04:36,909
Haven't I changed much compared to then?

114
00:04:36,976 --> 00:04:38,745
-No, you're the same.
-I am?

115
00:04:38,811 --> 00:04:40,413
I've become quite calm.

116
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:43,249
I make sure I stop
once I've gotten enough airtime.

117
00:04:43,316 --> 00:04:46,252
-Chefs, you'd better be careful.
-I'll keep my eyes on you.

118
00:04:46,319 --> 00:04:48,121
-She's feisty.
-She is.

119
00:04:48,187 --> 00:04:51,891
I hear Jung-an also has
a personal connection with Sang-uk.

120
00:04:51,958 --> 00:04:54,427
His wife and I

121
00:04:54,494 --> 00:04:57,530
are really close.

122
00:04:57,597 --> 00:04:58,731
-Cha Ye-ryun.
-Yes, Ye-ryun.

123
00:04:58,798 --> 00:05:00,300
Were you in the same project?

124
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:01,968
We hung out in the same neighborhood.

125
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:06,739
-The same neighborhood.
-It was the same neighborhood.

126
00:05:06,806 --> 00:05:10,076
-The same bakeries and coffee shops.
-Bakeries.

127
00:05:10,143 --> 00:05:11,644
And we shopped for groceries together.

128
00:05:11,711 --> 00:05:14,580
Even back then, she cooked for me.

129
00:05:14,647 --> 00:05:16,149
It was <i>gamja-tang, </i>pork backbone stew.

130
00:05:16,215 --> 00:05:18,751
Was that about 20 years ago?

131
00:05:18,818 --> 00:05:20,386
It's been a long time.

132
00:05:20,453 --> 00:05:23,656
Well, I don't personally know
Sang-uk too well,

133
00:05:23,723 --> 00:05:26,292
but you can tell by the way he looks

134
00:05:26,359 --> 00:05:27,527
that he has lived for a long time.

135
00:05:27,593 --> 00:05:29,162
He's not immature.

136
00:05:29,228 --> 00:05:31,497
SUDDENLY ATTACKED FOR NO REASON

137
00:05:31,564 --> 00:05:35,301
She has a distinct way of talking,
so you have to hear her out.

138
00:05:35,368 --> 00:05:37,403
-I am a bit older.
-He has that vibe.

139
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:39,806
His eyes show
that he's a stable and grounded guy.

140
00:05:39,872 --> 00:05:43,042
Maybe it's because he often played
the king, but he looks composed

141
00:05:43,109 --> 00:05:45,144
-and very understanding.
-Yes, I can see that.

142
00:05:45,211 --> 00:05:48,014
Ye-ryun is someone
who's fierce like the fire,

143
00:05:48,081 --> 00:05:50,917
and it seemed like
you were someone who could embrace her.

144
00:05:51,184 --> 00:05:52,151
OH, A COMPLIMENT…

145
00:05:52,218 --> 00:05:54,554
-It was a compliment.
-After Jung-an's attack,

146
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,923
it seemed like Sang-uk

147
00:05:56,989 --> 00:05:59,492
-froze up a bit.
-It's not that.

148
00:05:59,559 --> 00:06:02,128
I wasn't sure
how to proceed with the answer.

149
00:06:02,428 --> 00:06:05,164
HE'S LOST
AFTER JUNG-AN'S UNPREDICTABLE STORY

150
00:06:05,231 --> 00:06:08,668
-I have a lot on my mind now.
-I see.

151
00:06:08,735 --> 00:06:11,003
Let me turn my body toward you.

152
00:06:11,070 --> 00:06:13,439
DIVERTING TO 1-ON-1 QUESTIONS
TO PROTECT THE GUEST

153
00:06:13,506 --> 00:06:15,408
He was the biggest villain

154
00:06:15,475 --> 00:06:18,578
of the drama <i>Agent Kim Reactivated.</i>

155
00:06:18,644 --> 00:06:19,479
SHAKING HEAD

156
00:06:19,545 --> 00:06:23,683
The viewership reached
up to 23% nationwide.

157
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:26,552
-It was a huge hit.
-The total number of viewers on TV

158
00:06:26,619 --> 00:06:28,721
amounted to 25.63 million people.

159
00:06:28,788 --> 00:06:31,991
After Chef Jong-won watched the drama,

160
00:06:32,058 --> 00:06:33,960
he couldn't shake off one of the scenes.

161
00:06:34,026 --> 00:06:35,328
It was the potato scene.

162
00:06:35,395 --> 00:06:38,898
I'm not too scared
of live fish or even big animals.

163
00:06:39,565 --> 00:06:42,268
But I've recently been flinching
whenever I see potatoes.

164
00:06:42,535 --> 00:06:44,604
GAVE HIM POTATO TRAUMA

165
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:47,073
-Didn't someone suffer from a burn?
-We have someone…

166
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:51,077
-Egg.
-…who got hospitalized after eating eggs.

167
00:06:51,144 --> 00:06:51,978
I thought of him.

168
00:06:52,278 --> 00:06:54,180
NAM-NO DID SOMETHING THAT SCARY…

169
00:06:54,247 --> 00:06:56,115
Have you watched that scene, Nam-no?

170
00:06:56,182 --> 00:06:57,817
-I have.
-You have?

171
00:06:57,884 --> 00:06:59,719
You probably related to it.

172
00:06:59,786 --> 00:07:02,221
-No, he would've…
-But potatoes have to be hot.

173
00:07:02,288 --> 00:07:04,424
-Did you say, "Looks good"?
-He'd swallow it.

174
00:07:04,490 --> 00:07:06,526
-"Looks good"?
-He's unfazed.

175
00:07:06,592 --> 00:07:08,194
NAM-NO'S ON ANOTHER LEVEL

176
00:07:08,461 --> 00:07:09,295
EVEN BETTER(?)

177
00:07:09,362 --> 00:07:11,664
-Did you go on your reward trip?
-We did.

178
00:07:11,731 --> 00:07:14,300
We went to Guam for four days.

179
00:07:14,367 --> 00:07:16,636
Was everything paid for?

180
00:07:17,136 --> 00:07:18,771
WHAT THE VIEWERS MIGHT ASK

181
00:07:18,838 --> 00:07:20,273
-Was it free?
-It is a reward.

182
00:07:20,339 --> 00:07:21,641
Anything out of your pocket?

183
00:07:21,707 --> 00:07:23,142
"Out of my pocket"?

184
00:07:23,209 --> 00:07:25,511
-I'm sure he paid for the magnet.
-Magnets, sure.

185
00:07:25,578 --> 00:07:27,246
-The flight…
-The flight.

186
00:07:27,313 --> 00:07:28,781
-…and the accommodation.
-The hotel.

187
00:07:28,848 --> 00:07:30,716
And breakfast.

188
00:07:30,783 --> 00:07:32,718
-Was it included?
-It was.

189
00:07:32,785 --> 00:07:34,253
-Breakfast is important.
-It is.

190
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:37,623
I haven't heard the phrase "out of pocket"
in a while. Can this go on TV?

191
00:07:37,690 --> 00:07:39,091
It can't.

192
00:07:39,158 --> 00:07:43,763
Many actors were dearly loved
through <i>Agent Kim Reactivated.</i>

193
00:07:43,830 --> 00:07:46,599
Another one among them was Yoon Kyung-ho.

194
00:07:46,666 --> 00:07:49,735
He made a pledge to the viewers,

195
00:07:49,802 --> 00:07:53,606
<i>saying he'd stay silent for 13 hours</i>
<i>if the ratings surpassed 13%.</i>

196
00:07:53,673 --> 00:07:55,641
And the one who suggested this pledge

197
00:07:55,708 --> 00:07:58,077
was you, Sang-uk? What's this about?

198
00:07:58,144 --> 00:07:59,278
He did?

199
00:07:59,345 --> 00:08:02,415
Asking for a viewership pledge
is a go-to interview question.

200
00:08:02,482 --> 00:08:04,684
-And Kyung-ho's quite chatty.
-He is.

201
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:06,586
AND SO, HE MADE THE PLEDGE

202
00:08:06,652 --> 00:08:09,088
And it actually went way over
after two episodes.

203
00:08:09,155 --> 00:08:10,990
-It went way over.
-Yes.

204
00:08:11,057 --> 00:08:13,459
He even had to stay silent
on a radio show.

205
00:08:13,526 --> 00:08:16,996
YOON KYUNG-HO'S SWORN SILENCE…
COMMUNICATES VIA WHITEBOARD

206
00:08:17,063 --> 00:08:20,032
And Sang-uk also suggested a pledge

207
00:08:20,099 --> 00:08:22,335
for his episode of the show

208
00:08:22,401 --> 00:08:25,137
-to our production crew.
-He did?

209
00:08:25,204 --> 00:08:26,239
They asked me.

210
00:08:26,305 --> 00:08:29,775
They wanted me
to pledge something for this episode,

211
00:08:29,842 --> 00:08:32,044
so I suggested 5%.

212
00:08:32,111 --> 00:08:34,580
-That'd be huge. 5%?
-Did you say 5%?

213
00:08:34,647 --> 00:08:35,781
Then they asked what I'd do.

214
00:08:35,848 --> 00:08:39,819
So since it's <i>Chef & My Fridge,</i>
I said I'll buy a fridge for everyone.

215
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:41,454
Really?

216
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:42,622
I needed a new fridge.

217
00:08:42,688 --> 00:08:45,658
-Everyone in here?
-Everyone here?

218
00:08:45,725 --> 00:08:47,126
-A regular one?
-A kimchi fridge?

219
00:08:47,193 --> 00:08:49,395
Things are becoming way too serious.

220
00:08:49,595 --> 00:08:50,429
EVERYONE IS SERIOUS

221
00:08:50,496 --> 00:08:52,698
-I needed a kimchi fridge.
-We're dead serious.

222
00:08:52,765 --> 00:08:54,867
I mean, it's not easy to reach.

223
00:08:54,934 --> 00:08:56,569
How about real-time viewership?

224
00:08:56,636 --> 00:08:58,771
Real-time? Oh, I don't think so.

225
00:08:59,105 --> 00:09:01,107
CHAIRMAN JOO
WON'T HAVE REAL-TIME VIEWERSHIP

226
00:09:01,173 --> 00:09:03,209
-That fluctuates.
-We'd have a chance.

227
00:09:03,276 --> 00:09:05,044
So not real-time viewership?

228
00:09:05,645 --> 00:09:07,046
It's too dangerous.

229
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:10,449
CHAIRMAN JOO KNOWS A THING OR TWO
ABOUT THE VIEWERSHIP GRAPH

230
00:09:10,516 --> 00:09:11,584
We could've gotten one.

231
00:09:11,651 --> 00:09:13,419
I'M GETTING THIRSTY…

232
00:09:13,486 --> 00:09:16,122
Well, it can happen
if our viewers give us a hand.

233
00:09:16,188 --> 00:09:18,991
We'll draw lots and give my fridge away
to one of our viewers.

234
00:09:19,292 --> 00:09:21,260
ONE OF THESE CAN BE YOURS♥

235
00:09:21,327 --> 00:09:22,228
I'm taking mine.

236
00:09:22,295 --> 00:09:24,363
Jung-an, will you also make a pledge

237
00:09:24,430 --> 00:09:26,866
for your upcoming episode?

238
00:09:26,933 --> 00:09:29,268
Sang-uk pledged fridges.

239
00:09:29,335 --> 00:09:31,437
Send us on a reward trip.

240
00:09:31,504 --> 00:09:34,473
-That's nice. Even to Gangwon-do.
-A reward trip?

241
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:36,509
An overseas trip can get pricey.

242
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:37,443
Jeju-do?

243
00:09:37,944 --> 00:09:39,412
JEJU-DO?

244
00:09:39,478 --> 00:09:41,247
Not even for long. Over two days.

245
00:09:41,314 --> 00:09:44,116
Isn't it too tiring
to take a two-day trip? Is it okay?

246
00:09:44,183 --> 00:09:45,017
Can we go longer?

247
00:09:45,084 --> 00:09:47,587
-I can do three days.
-Three days.

248
00:09:47,653 --> 00:09:49,855
-The flight and the hotel.
-Of course.

249
00:09:49,922 --> 00:09:52,091
-I can do that.
-Breakfast included?

250
00:09:52,658 --> 00:09:53,626
Breakfast?

251
00:09:53,693 --> 00:09:55,828
-I can go and make it for you.
-Not a bad idea.

252
00:09:55,895 --> 00:09:58,230
-Are you coming? Can you?
-Aren't I?

253
00:09:58,297 --> 00:10:00,232
-We'd love to have you.
-That'd be great.

254
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:01,968
You're coming too.

255
00:10:02,868 --> 00:10:04,303
-Me?
-Sang-uk's coming too.

256
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:05,304
-I am?
-You are.

257
00:10:05,871 --> 00:10:07,440
His facial expression changed.

258
00:10:07,707 --> 00:10:09,542
I'M CONFUSED…

259
00:10:09,609 --> 00:10:11,911
-Sang-uk, you're coming too.
-Let's go.

260
00:10:11,978 --> 00:10:14,280
-What was the number you called?
-I haven't said it yet.

261
00:10:14,347 --> 00:10:16,315
I'd say…

262
00:10:16,382 --> 00:10:17,316
How about 6.5%?

263
00:10:17,383 --> 00:10:19,652
-So it's 6.5%? Not 6% or 7%?
-It's 6.5%.

264
00:10:20,086 --> 00:10:21,754
HMM…

265
00:10:21,821 --> 00:10:24,624
If you reach 7%,
I'll send you on an overseas trip.

266
00:10:24,824 --> 00:10:27,393
LET'S TRAVEL ABROAD♥

267
00:10:27,460 --> 00:10:29,395
They're so generous.

268
00:10:29,595 --> 00:10:31,664
NOTHING OUT OF OUR POCKETS♥

269
00:10:31,731 --> 00:10:33,966
Let's go abroad.

270
00:10:34,033 --> 00:10:36,335
-So generous.
-When you cook today,

271
00:10:36,402 --> 00:10:37,837
be ready to give up your hands.

272
00:10:38,170 --> 00:10:41,273
COOK WITH EVERYTHING YOU GOT…

273
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:43,809
-Nam-no, just swallow a hot potato.
-But we need our viewers' help.

274
00:10:43,876 --> 00:10:46,012
-And lose your teeth.
-To get to 7%,

275
00:10:46,078 --> 00:10:48,147
he'd have to lose his teeth
after eating a potato.

276
00:10:48,214 --> 00:10:49,448
He'll have to do that.

277
00:10:49,515 --> 00:10:51,050
I'll eat something steaming hot.

278
00:10:51,117 --> 00:10:52,518
WE SAY THIS, BUT NEVER GET TO GO…

279
00:10:52,585 --> 00:10:53,919
Sang-uk is famous

280
00:10:53,986 --> 00:10:56,989
-for his love of fishing.
-Fishing.

281
00:10:57,056 --> 00:11:01,961
So we've prepared a few pictures,
provided by Sang-uk himself.

282
00:11:02,028 --> 00:11:03,195
Show us.

283
00:11:03,262 --> 00:11:05,164
THE FISHING PICTURES!

284
00:11:05,231 --> 00:11:07,266
-He's like a professional fisherman.
-What's going on?

285
00:11:07,333 --> 00:11:08,300
Are those squid?

286
00:11:08,367 --> 00:11:11,037
-What's that?
-Bigfin reef squid!

287
00:11:11,103 --> 00:11:12,972
-Tell us about it.
-They're huge.

288
00:11:13,739 --> 00:11:15,574
-That's flatfish.
-Flatfish.

289
00:11:15,641 --> 00:11:18,110
A jumbo flatfish I caught in Gangneung.

290
00:11:18,177 --> 00:11:19,378
-Jumbo.
-That's like ten kg.

291
00:11:19,445 --> 00:11:21,514
It was about one m, ten cm.

292
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:23,349
You can catch that?

293
00:11:23,416 --> 00:11:24,984
A little smaller than me.

294
00:11:25,051 --> 00:11:26,919
Is that a bream next to that?

295
00:11:26,986 --> 00:11:28,454
-Rock bream.
-Rock bream.

296
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:29,422
That costs a fortune.

297
00:11:29,488 --> 00:11:31,323
I've caught many that were much bigger,

298
00:11:31,390 --> 00:11:33,092
because that's not quite 60 cm.

299
00:11:33,159 --> 00:11:35,461
-It was about 58 cm.
-About 58 cm.

300
00:11:35,528 --> 00:11:38,998
Next to that is in season right now,
bigfin reef squid.

301
00:11:39,065 --> 00:11:41,000
-It's that big?
-Whoa.

302
00:11:41,067 --> 00:11:42,702
-It's so big.
-That one…

303
00:11:42,768 --> 00:11:44,770
-…was 1.5 kg.
-That was 1.5 kg.

304
00:11:44,837 --> 00:11:46,672
-It was about 1.5 kg.
-That's 200,000 won.

305
00:11:46,739 --> 00:11:48,107
That cod is so…

306
00:11:48,174 --> 00:11:50,109
-That cod.
-Cod.

307
00:11:50,176 --> 00:11:52,545
It was about 1 m, 20 cm.

308
00:11:52,611 --> 00:11:53,879
As big as a child.

309
00:11:53,946 --> 00:11:54,914
It must be about 18 kg.

310
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:56,682
Has your wife ever gone with you?

311
00:11:56,749 --> 00:11:59,652
She likes to fish too,
so she often comes along.

312
00:11:59,719 --> 00:12:02,688
<i>She's good at fishing on a boat.</i>
<i>She catches things like bigfin reef squid.</i>

313
00:12:02,755 --> 00:12:03,923
I see.

314
00:12:03,989 --> 00:12:05,791
-Do you clean and prep them?
-I do.

315
00:12:05,858 --> 00:12:08,627
I'd prep them the day before,
and early next evening,

316
00:12:08,694 --> 00:12:11,564
I'd invite the usual members,
between six to eight people,

317
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:13,232
and have a party.

318
00:12:13,299 --> 00:12:15,601
-Does Ye-ryun cook?
-Yes.

319
00:12:15,668 --> 00:12:17,570
She's in charge of cooking.

320
00:12:17,636 --> 00:12:18,871
I fillet the raw fish,

321
00:12:18,938 --> 00:12:21,807
<i>but my wife</i>
<i>mostly makes </i>jjigae <i>and fritters.</i>

322
00:12:21,874 --> 00:12:22,975
That sounds so good.

323
00:12:23,042 --> 00:12:26,612
-She's famous as a good cook.
-Yes, she's famous.

324
00:12:26,679 --> 00:12:29,849
So much so that
she's nicknamed Cha Jang-geum,

325
00:12:29,915 --> 00:12:31,383
like Jang-geum of <i>Jewel in the Palace.</i>

326
00:12:31,450 --> 00:12:33,753
She already loves to cook,

327
00:12:33,819 --> 00:12:37,623
and she posts cooking videos
on her channel every week.

328
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:42,695
So our fridge
is always full and organized.

329
00:12:42,762 --> 00:12:45,698
We're looking forward
to seeing what's in your fridge.

330
00:12:45,765 --> 00:12:49,969
A fridge used by an angler
and Cha Jang-geum.

331
00:12:50,035 --> 00:12:51,570
You have a daughter too, right?

332
00:12:51,637 --> 00:12:53,105
-Yes.
-How old is she now?

333
00:12:53,172 --> 00:12:55,241
-Nine.
-Nine.

334
00:12:55,307 --> 00:12:57,610
-The three of us.
-The family.

335
00:12:57,676 --> 00:12:59,178
I'm jealous

336
00:12:59,245 --> 00:13:02,281
that he gets to eat the food
made by someone like Ye-ryun,

337
00:13:02,348 --> 00:13:06,018
and that he can share a fridge with her.

338
00:13:06,085 --> 00:13:07,920
But isn't your life too abundant?

339
00:13:08,921 --> 00:13:10,723
-You live in abundance.
-My life?

340
00:13:10,790 --> 00:13:14,560
What exactly do you mean
by living an "abundant" life?

341
00:13:14,627 --> 00:13:16,595
Do you mean that they make good money?

342
00:13:16,662 --> 00:13:18,764
-Or do they eat in abundance?
-That's funny.

343
00:13:18,831 --> 00:13:21,867
What's in your fridge
shows what your life is like, right?

344
00:13:21,934 --> 00:13:23,769
I am living an abundant life.

345
00:13:23,836 --> 00:13:26,372
Also, he has that rich guy's face.

346
00:13:26,438 --> 00:13:28,040
-Rich guy's face?
-Rich guy?

347
00:13:28,107 --> 00:13:29,308
AGREED

348
00:13:29,375 --> 00:13:30,209
He smells like money.

349
00:13:30,442 --> 00:13:31,677
A STRANGE COMPLIMENT(?)

350
00:13:31,744 --> 00:13:33,813
She's throwing random things out there.

351
00:13:33,879 --> 00:13:36,081
That's better than looking poor.

352
00:13:36,148 --> 00:13:38,350
-It's good that you look rich.
-It is.

353
00:13:38,417 --> 00:13:40,452
Yes, we do live in abundance.

354
00:13:40,519 --> 00:13:43,489
-That's why he plays the king.
-That's true.

355
00:13:43,556 --> 00:13:44,957
My neck suddenly got stiff.

356
00:13:45,024 --> 00:13:46,225
Why?

357
00:13:46,292 --> 00:13:47,960
JOKING

358
00:13:48,027 --> 00:13:50,062
I wonder what Sang-uk, the rich guy,

359
00:13:50,129 --> 00:13:52,798
keeps in his fridge,
so why don't we find out?

360
00:13:52,865 --> 00:13:54,166
-Here we go.
-I'm curious.

361
00:13:54,233 --> 00:13:56,635
-We present Sang-uk's fridge!
-Fridge!

362
00:13:56,702 --> 00:13:57,603
-…fridge!
-Fridge!

363
00:13:57,670 --> 00:13:58,604
Large yellowtail.

364
00:13:59,505 --> 00:14:02,508
MURMURING

365
00:14:02,842 --> 00:14:05,144
PRESENTING SANG-UK'S FRIDGE

366
00:14:05,211 --> 00:14:08,347
-I don't see any fish.
-The freezer's empty.

367
00:14:08,414 --> 00:14:10,816
-Where's the squid?
-I expected it to be full of fish.

368
00:14:10,883 --> 00:14:11,784
Wait a second.

369
00:14:11,851 --> 00:14:12,852
AWKWARD(?)

370
00:14:12,918 --> 00:14:16,555
-Where's the large yellowtail?
-I thought I'd see some tilefish.

371
00:14:16,622 --> 00:14:18,657
-I thought I'd see some fish.
-Let's take a look.

372
00:14:18,724 --> 00:14:20,993
We'll go through everything one by one.

373
00:14:21,060 --> 00:14:23,128
I see why she's called Cha Jang-geum.

374
00:14:23,195 --> 00:14:26,765
-Everything looks so neat.
-It's so neat.

375
00:14:26,832 --> 00:14:29,034
The first shelf.

376
00:14:29,101 --> 00:14:31,337
See? He does eat raw fish.

377
00:14:31,403 --> 00:14:34,206
-See? Ginger and <i>chogochujang</i>.
-<i>Chogochujang </i>and ginger.

378
00:14:34,273 --> 00:14:36,976
-This is a huge pack of wasabi.
-That's a good one.

379
00:14:37,042 --> 00:14:38,677
This is 200 g.

380
00:14:38,744 --> 00:14:40,479
-You're right.
-Is that expensive?

381
00:14:40,546 --> 00:14:42,781
-It is, right?
-She suddenly asks if it's expensive.

382
00:14:42,848 --> 00:14:45,551
-He lives in abundance.
-He does.

383
00:14:45,618 --> 00:14:46,852
HEHE

384
00:14:46,919 --> 00:14:48,053
And next to that,

385
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:50,422
I see some beer. Do you enjoy beer?

386
00:14:50,489 --> 00:14:53,292
Sometimes I need something more
even after I grab a drink outside.

387
00:14:54,526 --> 00:14:55,527
To wash it down.

388
00:14:55,594 --> 00:14:57,263
But it's not what you usually drink?

389
00:14:57,329 --> 00:15:00,532
-It's like a beverage.
-A beverage?

390
00:15:00,599 --> 00:15:02,067
-You must like to drink.
-I keep it there.

391
00:15:02,134 --> 00:15:06,805
-I personally like soju the most.
-I see.

392
00:15:06,872 --> 00:15:09,875
I can drink about two or three bottles.

393
00:15:09,942 --> 00:15:12,111
If he says two or three,
it actually means five.

394
00:15:12,177 --> 00:15:13,646
And you don't have a drunk habit?

395
00:15:13,712 --> 00:15:15,948
I don't. But when I get drunk…

396
00:15:16,015 --> 00:15:17,883
I'm actually quite chatty.

397
00:15:17,950 --> 00:15:19,184
REALLY?

398
00:15:19,752 --> 00:15:21,287
But today…

399
00:15:21,587 --> 00:15:23,088
BECAUSE OF SOMEONE…

400
00:15:23,155 --> 00:15:24,924
He's here with someone more chatty.

401
00:15:24,990 --> 00:15:26,625
I'm actually surprised at myself.

402
00:15:26,692 --> 00:15:28,060
Have I always been this calm?

403
00:15:29,695 --> 00:15:32,965
I get chatty when I drink.
Even more so than usual.

404
00:15:33,032 --> 00:15:34,733
-Do you become more energetic?
-Yes.

405
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:36,335
I see alcohol over here.

406
00:15:36,402 --> 00:15:38,404
Soju, champagne, wine…

407
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:40,239
He's had quite a lot already.

408
00:15:40,306 --> 00:15:42,207
He only has a little left.

409
00:15:42,274 --> 00:15:43,742
You have a wine cellar?

410
00:15:43,809 --> 00:15:45,311
He lives in abundance.

411
00:15:46,111 --> 00:15:48,113
How abundant. Lady Gaga vintage.

412
00:15:48,180 --> 00:15:49,748
You live in abundance.

413
00:15:49,815 --> 00:15:53,018
O WINE, A PREMIUM BOTTLE
FROM NAPA VALLEY, USA

414
00:15:53,085 --> 00:15:55,354
Jung-an's prediction was accurate.

415
00:15:55,421 --> 00:15:57,323
What kind of bar snacks do you enjoy?

416
00:15:57,389 --> 00:15:58,324
Here's the thing.

417
00:15:58,857 --> 00:16:01,727
I never start with the drink
and then choose the food.

418
00:16:01,794 --> 00:16:05,164
I'm the type who thinks of the drink
when I see a certain type of food.

419
00:16:05,230 --> 00:16:07,266
So depending on what my dinner is,

420
00:16:07,333 --> 00:16:10,336
I go with wine, soju, or something else.

421
00:16:10,703 --> 00:16:12,237
PICKS THE DRINK AFTER THE FOOD

422
00:16:12,304 --> 00:16:14,440
And some side dishes over here.

423
00:16:14,506 --> 00:16:18,844
Look at how Cha Jang-geum prepped this.

424
00:16:18,911 --> 00:16:22,114
Green onion and onion
are prepped neatly in silicone bags.

425
00:16:22,181 --> 00:16:24,483
It looks like an actual bag.

426
00:16:24,550 --> 00:16:25,651
Right?

427
00:16:25,718 --> 00:16:27,720
-And this one too. It's a clutch bag.
-It's a bag.

428
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:28,988
2026 F/W SILICONE BAG

429
00:16:29,054 --> 00:16:29,888
So cute.

430
00:16:29,955 --> 00:16:31,457
I didn't know these were a thing.

431
00:16:31,523 --> 00:16:33,392
-These are garlic cloves.
-Garlic.

432
00:16:33,459 --> 00:16:37,229
What's this powder underneath?

433
00:16:37,296 --> 00:16:40,599
What could it be? Powder?

434
00:16:40,666 --> 00:16:43,402
-It looks like sugar to me.
-Sugar?

435
00:16:43,469 --> 00:16:45,504
That's how you should keep garlic.

436
00:16:45,571 --> 00:16:46,672
Is that so?

437
00:16:46,739 --> 00:16:49,942
Garlic has lots of moisture,
so it gets spoiled quickly.

438
00:16:50,009 --> 00:16:52,277
But sugar will absorb the moisture

439
00:16:52,344 --> 00:16:54,780
and keep the garlic fresh.

440
00:16:54,847 --> 00:16:56,081
By placing them on sugar?

441
00:16:56,148 --> 00:16:59,852
It sounds like Cha Jang-geum
is quite the cook.

442
00:16:59,918 --> 00:17:03,322
What do you think is her best dish?

443
00:17:03,389 --> 00:17:05,891
Something like <i>dubu-jorim,</i> braised tofu.

444
00:17:05,958 --> 00:17:08,293
Or <i>dak-bokkeum-tang,</i>
spicy braised chicken.

445
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:10,496
Even <i>sundubu-jjigae,</i> soft tofu stew.

446
00:17:10,562 --> 00:17:12,564
-Lots of tofu dishes.
-<i>Kimchi-jjim</i> sounds good.

447
00:17:12,631 --> 00:17:13,999
I like those, and they're good too.

448
00:17:14,066 --> 00:17:16,568
When something's really good,
do you jump on her?

449
00:17:16,902 --> 00:17:18,203
SUDDENLY GOES OFF

450
00:17:18,270 --> 00:17:20,672
-Jumping on her.
-Right.

451
00:17:20,739 --> 00:17:22,474
I saw him kissing her.

452
00:17:22,541 --> 00:17:24,009
-He kissed her?
-Yes.

453
00:17:24,076 --> 00:17:26,578
I saw you kissing her in a video.

454
00:17:26,645 --> 00:17:29,615
-I do compliment her a lot.
-Lots of compliments.

455
00:17:29,681 --> 00:17:30,883
He will not look at me.

456
00:17:30,949 --> 00:17:32,384
He can't look at her.

457
00:17:34,119 --> 00:17:35,988
STILL WON'T LOOK AT HER

458
00:17:36,055 --> 00:17:38,757
She's giving us all kinds of tips.

459
00:17:38,824 --> 00:17:40,926
It feels like we have three hosts today.

460
00:17:40,993 --> 00:17:42,895
-These questions are so random.
-She's a ticking bomb.

461
00:17:42,961 --> 00:17:45,764
-She suddenly throws it out there.
-The things we couldn't ask.

462
00:17:45,831 --> 00:17:48,634
Think of how good her food is.
He'd want to kiss her.

463
00:17:48,700 --> 00:17:50,035
-I'm sure.
-Of course.

464
00:17:50,102 --> 00:17:51,570
AGREED(?)

465
00:17:52,371 --> 00:17:54,807
-Give her a kiss when the food is good.
-He does.

466
00:17:54,873 --> 00:17:56,775
I'LL DO MY BEST(?)

467
00:17:56,842 --> 00:17:58,410
The third shelf.

468
00:17:58,477 --> 00:17:59,812
He has some meat.

469
00:17:59,878 --> 00:18:02,014
Thin skirt, Korean strip loin,

470
00:18:02,081 --> 00:18:03,549
and Korean chateaubriand.

471
00:18:03,615 --> 00:18:04,950
What? Chateaubriand?

472
00:18:05,017 --> 00:18:06,952
-Chateaubriand?
-Chateaubriand, Korean beef.

473
00:18:07,019 --> 00:18:09,721
-What's this? Chicken.
-Chicken, I think.

474
00:18:09,788 --> 00:18:11,990
What's your favorite type of meat?

475
00:18:12,057 --> 00:18:15,260
I like thin skirt the most.

476
00:18:15,327 --> 00:18:17,763
-Beef thin skirt.
-Yes, that's my favorite.

477
00:18:17,830 --> 00:18:20,599
I like raw fish more than meat, though.

478
00:18:21,467 --> 00:18:24,002
I tend to lean toward Japanese cuisine.

479
00:18:24,069 --> 00:18:25,737
Anything you don't like?

480
00:18:25,804 --> 00:18:27,372
There's one thing. I can't eat cheese.

481
00:18:27,439 --> 00:18:29,508
-What?
-What? Cheese?

482
00:18:29,575 --> 00:18:30,476
IMMEDIATELY TEASES

483
00:18:30,542 --> 00:18:32,778
-You can't eat cheese?
-Western chefs are in trouble.

484
00:18:32,845 --> 00:18:35,414
-Emergency, lousy Western cooks.
-It sounds like trouble.

485
00:18:35,948 --> 00:18:37,149
What happens if you eat it?

486
00:18:38,717 --> 00:18:39,985
FLUSTERED…

487
00:18:40,052 --> 00:18:42,855
-We have three hosts today.
-I'm sorry.

488
00:18:42,921 --> 00:18:44,556
Are you allergic to it?

489
00:18:44,623 --> 00:18:47,726
-No, I'm not allergic to it.
-You just don't like it?

490
00:18:47,793 --> 00:18:50,496
-I can't stand that distinct smell.
-The musty smell.

491
00:18:50,562 --> 00:18:52,698
-It's tough to take.
-It's an emergency.

492
00:18:52,764 --> 00:18:55,701
Do you usually eat a big portion?

493
00:18:55,767 --> 00:18:59,638
-I can eat a bag of pasta for a meal.
-It's 500 g.

494
00:18:59,705 --> 00:19:00,839
That's a lot.

495
00:19:00,906 --> 00:19:03,475
-I can finish that.
-It's like five servings.

496
00:19:03,542 --> 00:19:04,543
-Five servings.
-Really?

497
00:19:04,610 --> 00:19:06,478
-Do you like noodle dishes?
-I love them.

498
00:19:06,545 --> 00:19:10,282
If someone told me to live off noodles,
I could do that.

499
00:19:10,349 --> 00:19:11,884
-Really?
-He likes noodles.

500
00:19:11,950 --> 00:19:14,019
-He has lots of noodle types here.
-He does.

501
00:19:14,086 --> 00:19:16,688
Buckwheat noodles, corn noodles,

502
00:19:16,755 --> 00:19:19,958
pasta, and thin wheat noodles.

503
00:19:20,025 --> 00:19:23,762
Any memorable noodle dishes
that you've had at any point in time?

504
00:19:23,829 --> 00:19:26,765
I went to Milan early this year,

505
00:19:26,832 --> 00:19:28,333
and it was my first time in Italy.

506
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:32,104
I made a reservation
at a famous restaurant and went there,

507
00:19:32,171 --> 00:19:33,772
and their pasta

508
00:19:33,839 --> 00:19:36,542
was just the noodles on the plate.

509
00:19:36,608 --> 00:19:37,843
What?

510
00:19:37,910 --> 00:19:40,946
I thought something was missing,
because it was just the noodles.

511
00:19:41,013 --> 00:19:41,847
I was like, "What is this?"

512
00:19:41,914 --> 00:19:43,482
I had a bite,

513
00:19:43,549 --> 00:19:46,385
and it tasted like it was made
with just olive oil, salt, and pasta.

514
00:19:46,885 --> 00:19:51,123
I could only taste
those three ingredients,

515
00:19:51,190 --> 00:19:54,092
but it was so good
that it was sealed into my memory.

516
00:19:54,159 --> 00:19:56,595
-What was that?
-That's not a thing.

517
00:19:56,662 --> 00:19:57,896
What if the server thought,

518
00:19:57,963 --> 00:19:59,665
"Wait, I haven't poured the sauce yet"?

519
00:19:59,731 --> 00:20:02,701
-So I ate it before it was done?
-"You had everything already?"

520
00:20:02,768 --> 00:20:05,938
-Was there something more? I loved it.
-Are you sure?

521
00:20:06,705 --> 00:20:09,241
Chef Sam, does this dish sound plausible?

522
00:20:09,308 --> 00:20:10,842
I don't know.

523
00:20:10,909 --> 00:20:13,879
If he didn't see any other ingredients,

524
00:20:13,946 --> 00:20:15,480
it must've been
a complicated dish to make.

525
00:20:15,547 --> 00:20:17,583
-Not even garlic?
-Maybe it's this.

526
00:20:17,649 --> 00:20:18,984
Not even garlic.

527
00:20:19,051 --> 00:20:20,118
-No?
-Not even the smell?

528
00:20:20,185 --> 00:20:23,355
I think I remember them
grating something on top.

529
00:20:23,422 --> 00:20:26,058
-A dash of cheese?
-Maybe. Other than that, just noodles.

530
00:20:26,291 --> 00:20:27,726
INTRIGUED

531
00:20:27,793 --> 00:20:30,362
-It was strange yet good.
-The best pasta.

532
00:20:30,429 --> 00:20:33,465
I see many kinds of soy sauce
on this side.

533
00:20:33,532 --> 00:20:36,735
I usually make
soy sauce noodles for my daughter,

534
00:20:38,103 --> 00:20:39,071
or egg <i>bibimbap</i> in soy sauce.

535
00:20:40,339 --> 00:20:41,240
For your daughter?

536
00:20:41,306 --> 00:20:44,109
Yes, and I'm in charge
of making those simple dishes.

537
00:20:45,244 --> 00:20:47,412
Which soy sauce do you use?

538
00:20:47,479 --> 00:20:49,014
-It's…
-Is this it?

539
00:20:49,081 --> 00:20:52,150
-That too, and the small one to the right.
-This one?

540
00:20:52,217 --> 00:20:54,886
-I see.
-It's the egg soy sauce.

541
00:20:54,953 --> 00:20:56,688
That one's a bit sweet.

542
00:20:56,755 --> 00:21:00,259
But it's delicious.
Even adults would like it.

543
00:21:00,325 --> 00:21:02,561
And I see these things over here.

544
00:21:02,628 --> 00:21:06,131
Caviar, truffle sauce, sea urchin roe,

545
00:21:06,198 --> 00:21:08,033
and <i>jamón.</i>

546
00:21:08,100 --> 00:21:12,471
It's a light snack I like to have
with friends, with some wine.

547
00:21:12,537 --> 00:21:13,438
CLASSY SNACK FOR GUESTS

548
00:21:13,505 --> 00:21:16,141
-They're bar snacks.
-They're good.

549
00:21:16,208 --> 00:21:18,443
Next, the freezer.

550
00:21:20,312 --> 00:21:21,847
You use really good meat.

551
00:21:21,913 --> 00:21:24,850
-He uses great meat.
-What is it?

552
00:21:24,916 --> 00:21:26,518
I'm the model.

553
00:21:26,585 --> 00:21:27,586
SHOWING OFF

554
00:21:27,653 --> 00:21:30,656
But they do have really good meat.

555
00:21:30,722 --> 00:21:32,157
Take a new picture.

556
00:21:32,224 --> 00:21:33,959
-Come on.
-Come on.

557
00:21:34,026 --> 00:21:35,060
You look like Bbo-sik in the photo.

558
00:21:35,127 --> 00:21:37,496
Wait, what's this?

559
00:21:37,562 --> 00:21:39,298
-Eel.
-Eel.

560
00:21:39,364 --> 00:21:41,166
Just think of everything as a bar snack.

561
00:21:41,233 --> 00:21:42,668
-Bar snack.
-They're all bar snacks.

562
00:21:42,734 --> 00:21:45,037
-You eat it with a drink?
-Yes, a simple bar snack.

563
00:21:45,103 --> 00:21:47,272
He likes to fish, but he has zero seafood.

564
00:21:47,339 --> 00:21:51,576
Right? All I found
was this ready-made eel.

565
00:21:51,643 --> 00:21:53,779
He must have a separate aquarium.

566
00:21:53,845 --> 00:21:55,514
-He must have one at home.
-An aquarium?

567
00:21:55,580 --> 00:21:57,382
-It'll be here.
-You can bring it in now.

568
00:21:57,449 --> 00:21:59,751
-Please bring it in.
-They must be alive.

569
00:21:59,818 --> 00:22:01,253
He catches them alive.

570
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:05,557
-We need to see the one-meter fish.
-Bigfin reef squid, cod, jumbo yellowtail.

571
00:22:05,624 --> 00:22:08,627
Anything? We need something here. No?

572
00:22:08,694 --> 00:22:09,861
-I guess not.
-Why not?

573
00:22:09,928 --> 00:22:12,564
-So I thought about it.
-You did?

574
00:22:12,631 --> 00:22:17,235
I considered catching
and bringing some bigfin reef squid.

575
00:22:17,302 --> 00:22:19,204
But the weather's still too hot,

576
00:22:19,271 --> 00:22:21,273
so I couldn't go fishing at all.

577
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:23,241
Whenever I catch something,

578
00:22:23,308 --> 00:22:25,043
I consume everything on that day.

579
00:22:25,110 --> 00:22:27,746
-Do you eat it all?
-Everything.

580
00:22:27,813 --> 00:22:30,649
So I was really dwelling over it.

581
00:22:30,716 --> 00:22:34,119
We aren't asking for us.

582
00:22:34,186 --> 00:22:35,220
You seem to love seafood,

583
00:22:35,287 --> 00:22:37,622
but you have to bring some
for them to cook with it.

584
00:22:37,689 --> 00:22:40,392
-It's such a shame. I…
-Well, he has some.

585
00:22:40,459 --> 00:22:41,793
Anchovies.

586
00:22:42,394 --> 00:22:43,795
HUMBLE…

587
00:22:43,862 --> 00:22:45,897
Nothing like abalone or turban snail?

588
00:22:45,964 --> 00:22:49,801
-I never eat abalone or turban snail.
-No?

589
00:22:49,868 --> 00:22:51,436
-Why not?
-I see those and think,

590
00:22:51,503 --> 00:22:53,672
"These are wonderful bait."

591
00:22:53,739 --> 00:22:55,340
-Bait.
-For fish.

592
00:22:55,407 --> 00:22:57,409
It's for fish, not for people.

593
00:22:57,476 --> 00:22:58,343
I see them as bait.

594
00:22:58,410 --> 00:23:01,880
I think,
"It feels like I'm eating fish food."

595
00:23:01,947 --> 00:23:03,215
PRECIOUS BAIT FOR ROCK BREAMS

596
00:23:03,281 --> 00:23:04,850
He can't help but think that.

597
00:23:04,916 --> 00:23:06,418
He has frozen shrimp.

598
00:23:06,485 --> 00:23:08,520
Some shrimp.

599
00:23:08,587 --> 00:23:11,022
-Is this bait?
-It must be bait.

600
00:23:11,089 --> 00:23:13,024
-They look too nice, though.
-They are.

601
00:23:13,091 --> 00:23:15,761
Anything frozen is pretty much bait.

602
00:23:15,827 --> 00:23:17,295
THE FROZEN SECTION IS MOSTLY BAIT(?)

603
00:23:17,362 --> 00:23:20,165
-It's like that.
-So he has a philosophy.

604
00:23:20,232 --> 00:23:21,533
For my wife's birthday,

605
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:25,704
I wanted to make her
a pretty plate of raw fish fillets.

606
00:23:25,771 --> 00:23:28,907
<i>So I went to Noryangjin, ran into a fan,</i>

607
00:23:28,974 --> 00:23:30,776
<i>and she poured me a basket of shrimp.</i>

608
00:23:31,576 --> 00:23:34,045
She poured it on top of the wrapping.

609
00:23:34,112 --> 00:23:34,946
Raw shrimp.

610
00:23:35,013 --> 00:23:37,349
They were on the house?

611
00:23:37,416 --> 00:23:39,084
Those are perfect

612
00:23:39,151 --> 00:23:41,620
for rockfish or…

613
00:23:43,088 --> 00:23:44,122
-Bait.
-It's bait.

614
00:23:44,189 --> 00:23:46,057
He really doesn't have any fish.

615
00:23:46,124 --> 00:23:48,593
We looked through
the fish-less angler's fridge

616
00:23:48,660 --> 00:23:52,097
that's only full of
Cha Jang-geum's touches.

617
00:23:52,164 --> 00:23:53,832
FINISHED CHECKING OUT SANG-UK'S FRIDGE!

618
00:23:53,899 --> 00:23:57,102
-It was too hot, so I couldn't go.
-I get it.

619
00:23:57,169 --> 00:24:00,071
What kind of dishes does he want to eat?

620
00:24:00,138 --> 00:24:02,374
Show us his orders.

621
00:24:02,441 --> 00:24:03,909
I wonder what they are.

622
00:24:03,975 --> 00:24:06,845
Glamorous Temptation for Cha Jang-geum.

623
00:24:06,912 --> 00:24:08,814
That's a fancy title.

624
00:24:08,880 --> 00:24:11,450
You two met
in the drama <i>Glamorous Temptation</i>.

625
00:24:11,516 --> 00:24:13,552
-You're right.
-That's why.

626
00:24:13,885 --> 00:24:17,155
A TOPIC ABOUT THEIR BEGINNING

627
00:24:17,222 --> 00:24:21,526
She always cooks for me,
and she makes so many things.

628
00:24:21,593 --> 00:24:24,563
So what if I were to cook for her?

629
00:24:24,629 --> 00:24:27,499
If you make me something, I'll learn,

630
00:24:28,066 --> 00:24:32,404
and make sure
I treat my wife to a beautiful meal.

631
00:24:32,471 --> 00:24:33,772
-She's a good cook.
-That's why.

632
00:24:33,839 --> 00:24:36,508
If you want to do that, please get ready.

633
00:24:36,575 --> 00:24:38,543
What's the next order?

634
00:24:39,945 --> 00:24:43,415
-Chairman Booze, Time for Some Soju.
-Chairman Booze, Time for Some Soju.

635
00:24:43,482 --> 00:24:46,318
The chairman is called Booze, not Joo.

636
00:24:46,384 --> 00:24:48,186
I want to eat something

637
00:24:48,253 --> 00:24:51,256
that makes me crave soju
even when I didn't feel like drinking.

638
00:24:51,323 --> 00:24:53,291
-I see. It sounds tough.
-A soju snack.

639
00:24:53,358 --> 00:24:54,559
Something like that.

640
00:24:54,626 --> 00:24:55,961
No matter how good it is,

641
00:24:56,027 --> 00:24:58,230
if he doesn't crave soju, it's no good.

642
00:24:58,296 --> 00:24:59,164
That's it.

643
00:24:59,231 --> 00:25:01,366
If the dish doesn't turn out right,

644
00:25:01,433 --> 00:25:04,135
Chairman Joo will feed them potatoes.

645
00:25:05,003 --> 00:25:06,738
-Steaming hot potatoes.
-Steaming hot.

646
00:25:06,805 --> 00:25:10,141
He'll change into a villain.

647
00:25:10,208 --> 00:25:12,878
Now, a dish for Cha Jang-geum.

648
00:25:12,944 --> 00:25:14,346
Any volunteers?

649
00:25:14,412 --> 00:25:16,948
-I'll go.
-I knew it. Chef Hyun-suk.

650
00:25:17,015 --> 00:25:18,984
He always makes pretty dishes.

651
00:25:19,050 --> 00:25:22,020
Your wife is Cha Jang-geum, a good cook,

652
00:25:22,087 --> 00:25:24,856
so you need to make something
with a complicated process,

653
00:25:24,923 --> 00:25:28,894
but I can give you tips
on how to do them in an easier way.

654
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:30,729
He's right.

655
00:25:30,795 --> 00:25:32,864
It'll make her go, "You made this?"

656
00:25:32,931 --> 00:25:35,500
-I'll do that.
-So Chef Hyun-suk is in.

657
00:25:35,567 --> 00:25:36,401
Choi Hyun-suk.

658
00:25:37,302 --> 00:25:38,570
KWON SUNG-JUN IS IN

659
00:25:38,637 --> 00:25:40,238
Sung-jun is jumping in.

660
00:25:40,305 --> 00:25:43,008
After all,
I'm the perfect husband material.

661
00:25:43,074 --> 00:25:44,376
-So…
-Husband material?

662
00:25:44,442 --> 00:25:46,177
A man who hopes to be a doting husband,

663
00:25:46,244 --> 00:25:48,980
-Choi Soo-jong of Sindang-dong.
-Choi Soo-jong of Sindang-dong.

664
00:25:49,047 --> 00:25:50,482
So I'll join the round.

665
00:25:50,549 --> 00:25:52,017
I've never been married,

666
00:25:52,083 --> 00:25:54,786
but I do want to cook for my future wife,
so I'll try that today.

667
00:25:54,853 --> 00:25:56,655
All right.

668
00:25:56,721 --> 00:25:58,823
-It's a mirror match.
-The future doting husband.

669
00:25:58,890 --> 00:26:00,792
-A mirror match.
-The future doting husband.

670
00:26:00,859 --> 00:26:03,428
The two glamorous show-offs.

671
00:26:03,495 --> 00:26:04,429
A mirror match.

672
00:26:04,496 --> 00:26:08,500
And the next one,
Chairman Booze, Time for Some Soju.

673
00:26:09,367 --> 00:26:10,802
I'll go.

674
00:26:10,869 --> 00:26:12,370
-I knew it.
-Ho-young.

675
00:26:12,437 --> 00:26:14,272
-I want soju already.
-You need to join this.

676
00:26:14,339 --> 00:26:15,473
It already sounds delicious.

677
00:26:15,540 --> 00:26:17,776
That's a topic I'm confident with.

678
00:26:18,009 --> 00:26:19,678
I BELIEVE IN YOU

679
00:26:19,744 --> 00:26:21,613
This show actually has a villain,

680
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:24,749
-and I'd like to fight that one.
-Isn't it Sung-jun?

681
00:26:24,816 --> 00:26:26,851
There were a few accidents in the kitchen.

682
00:26:26,918 --> 00:26:28,787
-Yes.
-Canine.

683
00:26:28,853 --> 00:26:29,854
Canine.

684
00:26:29,921 --> 00:26:32,357
-That's true.
-Me? It's me?

685
00:26:32,424 --> 00:26:34,025
Can we join it together?

686
00:26:34,092 --> 00:26:38,430
-That's up to Sam.
-If Chef Sam accepts, yes.

687
00:26:38,496 --> 00:26:40,532
I'll gladly accept his challenge.

688
00:26:41,566 --> 00:26:43,001
Is Ho-young a piece of cake?

689
00:26:43,068 --> 00:26:45,670
-But a snack for soju?
-You don't drink.

690
00:26:45,737 --> 00:26:47,706
But I used to, in the old days.

691
00:26:47,772 --> 00:26:49,274
He had up to five bottles.

692
00:26:49,908 --> 00:26:52,277
EVEN CHAIRMAN BOOZE IS SHOCKED

693
00:26:52,344 --> 00:26:54,212
He popped the cap with his canine.

694
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:57,282
CANINE'S GLAMOROUS PAST

695
00:26:57,349 --> 00:26:58,950
-I know I can do it.
-You do?

696
00:26:59,184 --> 00:27:00,085
EXPECTATIONS RISING

697
00:27:00,151 --> 00:27:01,519
Sam accepts the challenge.

698
00:27:01,586 --> 00:27:03,655
All right, then.

699
00:27:03,722 --> 00:27:06,391
Glamorous Temptation for Cha Jang-geum.

700
00:27:06,458 --> 00:27:09,628
Let's begin the round
between Hyun-suk and Sung-jun!

701
00:27:12,564 --> 00:27:16,935
<i>The first round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with Sang-uk's ingredients.</i>

702
00:27:17,002 --> 00:27:19,671
Baby Hyun-suk, Chef Kwon Sung-jun,

703
00:27:19,738 --> 00:27:23,475
versus elderly Sung-jun,
Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

704
00:27:23,541 --> 00:27:24,376
Those two.

705
00:27:24,442 --> 00:27:28,813
We can even call this
the hate-my-own-kind derby.

706
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:30,849
Chef Sung-jun is dreaming

707
00:27:30,915 --> 00:27:34,085
of getting revenge on Chef Hyun-suk today.

708
00:27:34,152 --> 00:27:36,421
-Revenge?
-Is there a specific reason?

709
00:27:36,488 --> 00:27:39,858
I was here as a guest
for the previous episode,

710
00:27:39,924 --> 00:27:41,459
and he cooked for me.

711
00:27:41,526 --> 00:27:44,062
I came to realize that you can use food

712
00:27:44,129 --> 00:27:45,296
to insult someone.

713
00:27:45,363 --> 00:27:47,565
<i>-He's right.</i>
<i>-That's true.</i>

714
00:27:47,632 --> 00:27:49,901
<i>He did that thing on the plate.</i>

715
00:27:49,968 --> 00:27:52,003
It was a personal attack.

716
00:27:52,070 --> 00:27:53,872
-And he was the fridge owner.
-Yes.

717
00:27:53,938 --> 00:27:57,342
How could he insult him?
That never happens on this show.

718
00:27:57,409 --> 00:27:58,610
MOVING AS ONE

719
00:27:58,677 --> 00:28:02,313
As they say,
love always flows down, never up.

720
00:28:02,380 --> 00:28:04,683
What he wanted was attention,

721
00:28:04,749 --> 00:28:08,319
but the kind of attention he wants
is for his coolness and looks.

722
00:28:08,386 --> 00:28:11,990
-That's a stretch. You can't have that.
-How is that possible?

723
00:28:12,057 --> 00:28:14,426
Once I took on the role as a grandpa,

724
00:28:14,492 --> 00:28:17,095
many people have shown me their love.

725
00:28:17,762 --> 00:28:20,532
-"Role"?
-That's a true craving for attention.

726
00:28:20,598 --> 00:28:22,500
He only says he wants attention.

727
00:28:22,567 --> 00:28:25,070
-You get chatty as you age.
-And he should only want that.

728
00:28:25,136 --> 00:28:26,571
-Don't try to be cool.
-He's talkative.

729
00:28:26,638 --> 00:28:28,173
Grandpas want attention.

730
00:28:28,239 --> 00:28:30,141
I'm a bit surprised.

731
00:28:30,208 --> 00:28:31,776
-How so?
-He talks a lot.

732
00:28:34,112 --> 00:28:37,282
-When is he going to start cooking?
-Even he caught on to it.

733
00:28:37,348 --> 00:28:39,317
I mean, we get it.

734
00:28:39,384 --> 00:28:42,020
-Does he do that every week?
-Yes, weekly.

735
00:28:42,087 --> 00:28:44,089
-I see.
-We get what you're saying.

736
00:28:44,155 --> 00:28:46,224
-That's torture.
-We get it.

737
00:28:47,058 --> 00:28:49,094
CHEF SUNG-JUN'S INGREDIENTS:
DRUMSTICK, BONELESS CHICKEN THIGH,

738
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:51,229
SAMGYETANG HERBS, NAPA CABBAGE,
BOK CHOY, ENOKI, PASTA, AND MORE

739
00:28:51,296 --> 00:28:54,365
First off, Chef Sung-jun.
What are you going to make?

740
00:28:54,432 --> 00:28:56,434
The dish I've prepared is this.

741
00:28:56,501 --> 00:28:58,837
I Chicken You, Cha Jang-geum, My Love.

742
00:28:58,903 --> 00:29:01,139
-I Chicken You, Cha Jang-geum, My Love.
-Chicken You?

743
00:29:01,206 --> 00:29:02,741
You're using chicken?

744
00:29:02,807 --> 00:29:04,776
First, I'll stuff
<i>samgyetang</i> chicken with risotto,

745
00:29:04,843 --> 00:29:07,679
deep-fry the chicken,

746
00:29:07,746 --> 00:29:09,581
and serve it with hearty soup.

747
00:29:09,647 --> 00:29:11,316
There's something this one chef does,

748
00:29:11,382 --> 00:29:13,251
and it's blending herbs and making oil.

749
00:29:13,318 --> 00:29:15,120
Wait, the one next to you?

750
00:29:15,186 --> 00:29:18,656
That and deep-frying enoki.
I'm going to try doing that.

751
00:29:18,723 --> 00:29:20,992
Those will be my garnishes.

752
00:29:21,059 --> 00:29:23,895
-Hyun-suk did risotto <i>samgyetang</i> too.
-That's true.

753
00:29:23,962 --> 00:29:25,964
His recipe is all from Chef Hyun-suk.

754
00:29:26,030 --> 00:29:28,266
-Right?
-He's taking it all.

755
00:29:28,333 --> 00:29:31,302
Why do you think there's the saying,
"The student has surpassed the master"?

756
00:29:31,369 --> 00:29:32,670
I should surpass the master

757
00:29:33,338 --> 00:29:36,040
and fly high to reach my dream.

758
00:29:36,107 --> 00:29:38,176
-That's why…
-So I begin a battle against myself.

759
00:29:38,243 --> 00:29:40,345
I'm here to fight against myself.

760
00:29:40,411 --> 00:29:42,647
-He's just like his master.
-All right.

761
00:29:42,714 --> 00:29:44,349
One last thing.

762
00:29:44,415 --> 00:29:47,986
That salt pasta you had in Milan.

763
00:29:48,052 --> 00:29:51,122
I'm sure it was made
with stock and garlic,

764
00:29:51,189 --> 00:29:53,158
but today, I'll use just salt.

765
00:29:54,125 --> 00:29:55,860
You're going to recreate that?

766
00:29:55,927 --> 00:29:57,862
-You will?
-The pasta from Milan?

767
00:29:57,929 --> 00:29:59,764
-With just salt.
-Napoli Matfia will make it.

768
00:29:59,831 --> 00:30:02,534
I'll use only salt and olive oil.

769
00:30:02,600 --> 00:30:04,269
That's like plain rice in Korean cuisine.

770
00:30:04,335 --> 00:30:05,870
-Salted rice.
-Salted rice.

771
00:30:05,937 --> 00:30:08,273
-Salted rice.
-It's some salt over plain rice.

772
00:30:08,339 --> 00:30:10,074
-With a drop of sesame oil.
-Yes.

773
00:30:10,642 --> 00:30:12,777
He probably has a hidden recipe.

774
00:30:12,844 --> 00:30:14,412
HOPING TO SEE A GLAMOROUS TIP

775
00:30:15,246 --> 00:30:16,614
CHEF HYUN-SUK'S INGREDIENTS:

776
00:30:16,681 --> 00:30:19,417
TENDERLOIN, CAVIAR,
TRUFFLE CARPACCIO, JAMÓN, WHITE WINE

777
00:30:19,484 --> 00:30:22,487
Chef Hyun-suk is up against him.
What are you making?

778
00:30:22,554 --> 00:30:24,589
This is the name of my dish.

779
00:30:25,156 --> 00:30:26,291
Choi-sband.

780
00:30:26,357 --> 00:30:27,792
-Choi-mpion?
-Choi Hyun-suk?

781
00:30:27,859 --> 00:30:30,528
-He did Choi-mpion before.
-He's done it.

782
00:30:30,595 --> 00:30:32,163
For last year's final round,

783
00:30:32,230 --> 00:30:35,366
-I made a Wellington and won.
-Abalone Wellington.

784
00:30:35,433 --> 00:30:37,001
<i>-You're doing that again?</i>
<i>-But this one</i>

785
00:30:37,068 --> 00:30:39,771
will make him the best husband,
the choice husband.

786
00:30:39,838 --> 00:30:40,805
Choi-sband.

787
00:30:40,872 --> 00:30:42,507
It's Choi-sband.

788
00:30:42,574 --> 00:30:44,375
Not Choi-mpion?

789
00:30:44,442 --> 00:30:46,911
-Choi-sband.
-The best husband, Choi-sband.

790
00:30:46,978 --> 00:30:49,614
-The choice husband, Choi-sband.
-Back then,

791
00:30:49,681 --> 00:30:51,382
I used abalone because there was no beef.

792
00:30:51,449 --> 00:30:54,219
This time, I'll make
the actual beef Wellington.

793
00:30:54,285 --> 00:30:55,353
Another Wellington?

794
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:57,355
-Well-returns?
-Well-returns.

795
00:30:57,422 --> 00:31:00,825
Beef Wellington is made by wrapping meat

796
00:31:00,892 --> 00:31:04,262
with prosciutto and duxelles.

797
00:31:04,329 --> 00:31:06,798
But I had to use abalones
because the guest didn't have those.

798
00:31:06,865 --> 00:31:09,100
Today, he has everything.

799
00:31:09,167 --> 00:31:11,603
I knew I had to make that for him
the moment I saw them.

800
00:31:11,669 --> 00:31:13,872
You might think
a Wellington is tough to make,

801
00:31:13,938 --> 00:31:18,142
but there's an easier recipe
to make a dish that looks fancy.

802
00:31:18,209 --> 00:31:20,645
I'll make that happen for you.

803
00:31:20,712 --> 00:31:22,780
It has to be something he can do too.

804
00:31:22,847 --> 00:31:25,717
-Yes, of course.
-It shouldn't be too complicated.

805
00:31:25,783 --> 00:31:28,219
You have to explain the steps as you cook.

806
00:31:28,286 --> 00:31:32,290
And now, let us begin the cook-off.

807
00:31:32,357 --> 00:31:35,260
Give us the timer for 15 minutes.

808
00:31:35,326 --> 00:31:37,161
-Wow.
-Whoa.

809
00:31:37,228 --> 00:31:39,898
They only get exactly 15 minutes.

810
00:31:39,964 --> 00:31:41,232
Look at them.

811
00:31:41,299 --> 00:31:43,001
They crossed arms at the same speed.

812
00:31:43,067 --> 00:31:46,237
They're way too much like each other.

813
00:31:46,304 --> 00:31:49,007
-They did the same thing under the light.
-Exactly the same.

814
00:31:50,008 --> 00:31:52,343
It's truly a mirror match.

815
00:31:52,944 --> 00:31:54,646
The husband material who's ready to marry,

816
00:31:54,712 --> 00:31:58,182
the self-proclaimed Choi Soo-jong
of Sindang-dong, Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

817
00:31:58,249 --> 00:32:00,551
He's lived under his wife's watch
for the last 28 years,

818
00:32:00,618 --> 00:32:04,188
the self-proclaimed Joo Sang-uk
of Jinjeop-eup, Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

819
00:32:04,255 --> 00:32:05,323
-Jinjeop-eup?
-Jinjeop-eup.

820
00:32:05,390 --> 00:32:07,859
-Who will be the more doting husband?
-Namyangju?

821
00:32:07,926 --> 00:32:11,496
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

822
00:32:12,530 --> 00:32:13,932
The timer begins.

823
00:32:14,098 --> 00:32:14,966
THE COOKING CLASS BEGINS

824
00:32:15,033 --> 00:32:17,368
First, add oil to the pan.

825
00:32:17,435 --> 00:32:19,737
Cut the meat
into smaller pieces than steak.

826
00:32:19,804 --> 00:32:21,806
Salt the pasta water generously.

827
00:32:21,873 --> 00:32:22,874
EYES MOVING

828
00:32:22,941 --> 00:32:24,208
-They're both explaining.
-Salt.

829
00:32:24,275 --> 00:32:27,578
-I'll add garlic now.
-Sprinkle the salt from up high.

830
00:32:27,645 --> 00:32:28,613
It's so hectic.

831
00:32:28,680 --> 00:32:30,715
Can you two take turns to explain?

832
00:32:30,782 --> 00:32:32,016
Your audio tracks overlap.

833
00:32:32,583 --> 00:32:33,851
Now, the important thing.

834
00:32:33,918 --> 00:32:36,421
Sprinkle the salt from up high

835
00:32:36,487 --> 00:32:37,455
to look even cooler.

836
00:32:37,689 --> 00:32:38,790
LIKE THIS

837
00:32:38,856 --> 00:32:40,792
He took a step back.

838
00:32:40,858 --> 00:32:42,193
HYUN-SUK THROWS IN RANDOM TIPS

839
00:32:42,260 --> 00:32:43,361
It's that easy.

840
00:32:45,396 --> 00:32:47,098
What's he trying to do?

841
00:32:47,165 --> 00:32:48,700
"It's that easy."

842
00:32:48,766 --> 00:32:51,269
Instead of powdered black pepper,

843
00:32:51,336 --> 00:32:54,138
use a pepper mill
to add more depth to the flavor

844
00:32:54,205 --> 00:32:57,075
and to look like you're a good cook.

845
00:32:57,141 --> 00:32:58,042
DON'T YOU THINK?

846
00:32:58,109 --> 00:32:59,644
To his wife, that is.

847
00:32:59,711 --> 00:33:01,446
1. SEASON BEEF AND GRILL IN AN OILED PAN

848
00:33:02,246 --> 00:33:04,682
THIS CLASS STARTS WITH GREEN ONION

849
00:33:05,316 --> 00:33:07,652
1. BOIL GREEN ONION
IN A POT AND A PRESSURE COOKER

850
00:33:07,719 --> 00:33:09,687
Salt the pasta water generously.

851
00:33:09,854 --> 00:33:10,855
PASTA WATER, BROTH

852
00:33:11,656 --> 00:33:12,924
I'll add lots of garlic.

853
00:33:15,426 --> 00:33:16,594
NAPA CABBAGE, BOK CHOY

854
00:33:17,528 --> 00:33:18,863
AND DRUMSTICKS

855
00:33:19,163 --> 00:33:19,998
JAMÓN

856
00:33:20,965 --> 00:33:23,334
Make sure you add leftover <i>jamón</i>
when you boil the broth.

857
00:33:23,401 --> 00:33:25,069
<i>-Jamón?</i>
<i>-Jamón?</i>

858
00:33:25,136 --> 00:33:26,537
You added <i>jamón?</i>

859
00:33:26,604 --> 00:33:28,106
It makes the broth very delicious.

860
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:31,209
<i>Jamón</i> for the broth?

861
00:33:31,275 --> 00:33:32,110
SAMGYETANG HERBS

862
00:33:32,677 --> 00:33:34,479
<i>-Samgyetang </i>herbs.
<i>-Samgyetang </i>ingredients.

863
00:33:34,545 --> 00:33:36,147
It's for the broth.

864
00:33:36,214 --> 00:33:38,016
Why did you add <i>jamón?</i>

865
00:33:38,082 --> 00:33:41,352
It has a lot of umami,
so it's great for broth.

866
00:33:41,419 --> 00:33:44,322
-I see.
-If you don't want to eat leftover <i>jamón,</i>

867
00:33:44,389 --> 00:33:45,323
use it for broth.

868
00:33:45,390 --> 00:33:46,758
-Old <i>jamón.</i>
-They go dry.

869
00:33:46,824 --> 00:33:48,826
Now, the most important tip.

870
00:33:49,394 --> 00:33:51,329
I'll make the pie dough now.

871
00:33:51,896 --> 00:33:53,431
And when you make it,

872
00:33:53,498 --> 00:33:56,734
you're going to use butter instead.

873
00:33:56,801 --> 00:33:58,669
Blanch the green onion to make the oil.

874
00:33:58,736 --> 00:34:00,972
-Add cuts…
-Wait.

875
00:34:01,039 --> 00:34:03,674
Imagine that
you're kneading flour with butter.

876
00:34:03,741 --> 00:34:05,977
-Add oil to the green onion.
-Oil and green onion.

877
00:34:06,044 --> 00:34:09,313
It's hard to knead the dough by hand,
so just use a food processor.

878
00:34:09,547 --> 00:34:10,381
CONFUSION

879
00:34:10,448 --> 00:34:11,783
He'll get the recipes mixed up!

880
00:34:12,583 --> 00:34:13,551
Speak one at a time.

881
00:34:13,751 --> 00:34:14,786
BUTTER, EGG YOLK, WATER

882
00:34:14,852 --> 00:34:17,355
You only need one egg yolk.

883
00:34:17,422 --> 00:34:21,159
It's hard to knead the dough by hand,
so just use a food processor.

884
00:34:21,893 --> 00:34:22,894
Is that burning?

885
00:34:23,261 --> 00:34:24,128
STEAK'S BURNING, SIR

886
00:34:24,195 --> 00:34:26,230
-It'll get burnt.
-It's burning.

887
00:34:26,931 --> 00:34:28,833
I've oiled the pan generously,

888
00:34:28,900 --> 00:34:31,002
-so it will not burn.
-I see.

889
00:34:31,069 --> 00:34:33,538
Just flip it over.

890
00:34:33,604 --> 00:34:35,273
Isn't it seared perfectly?

891
00:34:35,339 --> 00:34:37,575
Cooking, it's that easy.

892
00:34:37,975 --> 00:34:39,177
EASY, GET IT?

893
00:34:39,811 --> 00:34:42,080
He's determined to win today.

894
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:43,881
CLASS OF THE MONTH: CHOI HYUN-SUK

895
00:34:43,948 --> 00:34:46,651
We might end up getting those fridges.

896
00:34:46,717 --> 00:34:48,419
Can you also do, "It's that easy"?

897
00:34:49,787 --> 00:34:50,755
It's hecka easy.

898
00:34:51,689 --> 00:34:52,790
He has trouble explaining.

899
00:34:52,857 --> 00:34:54,292
WHAT'S SUNG-JUN UP TO?

900
00:34:54,358 --> 00:34:56,394
Blanch the green onion to make the oil.

901
00:34:57,061 --> 00:34:57,995
Then squeeze the water out.

902
00:34:58,830 --> 00:35:00,765
He blanched the green onion to make oil.

903
00:35:01,032 --> 00:35:03,468
2. BLEND BLANCHED GREEN ONION,
OLIVE OIL, AND COOKING OIL

904
00:35:03,534 --> 00:35:04,402
Start.

905
00:35:07,171 --> 00:35:08,639
Why are you using <i>ramyeon</i> soup base?

906
00:35:08,706 --> 00:35:11,309
Because this one chef I know loves it.

907
00:35:11,375 --> 00:35:13,744
-It's an homage.
-For deep-fried enoki?

908
00:35:13,811 --> 00:35:16,647
-It's for the color.
-It's the same recipe.

909
00:35:16,714 --> 00:35:19,650
3. COAT ENOKI WITH STARCH
AND RAMYEON SOUP BASE, THEN DEEP-FRY

910
00:35:20,885 --> 00:35:21,719
All right.

911
00:35:22,153 --> 00:35:22,987
NEXT, FRIED CHICKEN

912
00:35:23,654 --> 00:35:24,489
SALT

913
00:35:24,922 --> 00:35:26,090
I have to warm up the rice.

914
00:35:26,424 --> 00:35:27,959
INSTANT RICE, INTO THE OVEN

915
00:35:28,025 --> 00:35:30,094
The pie dough is finished.

916
00:35:30,161 --> 00:35:33,131
The dough comes out nice and soft
if you knead it with butter.

917
00:35:33,197 --> 00:35:36,567
Pie dough is very soft,

918
00:35:36,634 --> 00:35:38,636
so you just have to roll it out gently.

919
00:35:39,203 --> 00:35:42,340
And then, cut it out like this.

920
00:35:42,406 --> 00:35:43,741
EASILY FINISHED MAKING THE PIE SHELL

921
00:35:43,808 --> 00:35:46,577
It's much easier to roll out
than <i>sujebi</i> dough.

922
00:35:46,644 --> 00:35:48,346
Isn't that easy?

923
00:35:48,613 --> 00:35:50,548
WHAT'S WITH THAT TONE?

924
00:35:50,615 --> 00:35:51,883
Who are you copying?

925
00:35:52,450 --> 00:35:54,685
Mr. Bob.

926
00:35:54,752 --> 00:35:55,853
Mr. Bob Ross.

927
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:57,321
"It's that easy."

928
00:35:57,388 --> 00:35:59,023
AKA "IT'S THAT EASY" GUY

929
00:35:59,090 --> 00:36:01,459
Rub butter all over the tray.

930
00:36:01,526 --> 00:36:02,693
-Rub it.
-It's not hard

931
00:36:02,760 --> 00:36:05,129
to rub butter on this. Just slap it on.

932
00:36:05,196 --> 00:36:06,497
-Slap it on.
-That's it.

933
00:36:06,564 --> 00:36:08,466
It's been three minutes.

934
00:36:08,533 --> 00:36:10,635
All lights are shining down on him.

935
00:36:10,701 --> 00:36:12,904
Sang-uk doesn't like talkative people.

936
00:36:12,970 --> 00:36:15,873
But I need to tell him about the recipe.

937
00:36:15,940 --> 00:36:17,675
-It all begins with the basics.
-The pasta.

938
00:36:17,742 --> 00:36:19,377
-Pasta.
-Pasta goes in.

939
00:36:19,443 --> 00:36:20,945
He said he'd only season it with salt.

940
00:36:21,012 --> 00:36:22,914
-I'll cook it for three minutes.
-Just salt.

941
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:25,283
Let's see if he really uses
just oil and salt.

942
00:36:25,850 --> 00:36:27,084
BEGINS TO MAKE RISOTTO

943
00:36:27,151 --> 00:36:28,719
Is that truffle sauce?

944
00:36:31,122 --> 00:36:32,957
What's he doing with the rice?

945
00:36:33,024 --> 00:36:34,225
-Add a bit of butter.
-Butter.

946
00:36:34,292 --> 00:36:35,826
To the rice?

947
00:36:35,993 --> 00:36:38,396
4. STIR-FRY TRUFFLE CREAM SAUCE,
RICE, BUTTER, AND GROUND PEPPER

948
00:36:38,462 --> 00:36:41,032
I need to make the risotto
and cool it over ice immediately.

949
00:36:41,098 --> 00:36:42,800
You're making risotto?

950
00:36:42,867 --> 00:36:45,203
-I did.
-You're already done?

951
00:36:45,269 --> 00:36:46,737
It has to cool first.

952
00:36:46,804 --> 00:36:49,407
-He'll fry that?
-He'll wrap it in chicken and deep-fry it.

953
00:36:49,473 --> 00:36:51,275
Now, enoki.

954
00:36:51,342 --> 00:36:54,579
They're ready,
made with Chef Hyun-suk's recipe.

955
00:36:54,645 --> 00:36:56,280
Yes, some chef I know likes this.

956
00:36:56,514 --> 00:36:57,982
WHAT IS "SOME CHEF" DOING?

957
00:36:58,916 --> 00:37:01,652
Next, put the pie shell on the tray.

958
00:37:02,987 --> 00:37:05,156
Place down a few pieces of <i>jamón</i>.

959
00:37:05,223 --> 00:37:07,825
-Then put the steak down.
-Okay.

960
00:37:07,892 --> 00:37:11,362
-He's using <i>jamón.</i>
-More <i>jamón</i> on top.

961
00:37:11,429 --> 00:37:13,231
Look at this Swiss pink.

962
00:37:13,297 --> 00:37:15,099
Isn't this beautiful?

963
00:37:15,166 --> 00:37:16,334
What pink?

964
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:18,769
-Swiss pink? What?
-What is that?

965
00:37:18,836 --> 00:37:20,438
No idea.

966
00:37:20,504 --> 00:37:23,941
-This is the truffle paste you had.
-Can you leave me a little?

967
00:37:24,008 --> 00:37:26,644
Sam, you can just use the oil.

968
00:37:27,078 --> 00:37:28,212
HEHEHE

969
00:37:28,279 --> 00:37:30,648
Do you have enough time
to cook it in the oven?

970
00:37:30,715 --> 00:37:32,650
Yes, Wellingtons take forever.

971
00:37:32,717 --> 00:37:35,853
-It takes forever because…
-Five minutes left.

972
00:37:35,920 --> 00:37:37,622
The inside… It's only been five minutes.

973
