00:47:09,193 --> 00:47:13,831
-The flavors settled down.
-It changed completely with caviar.

1218
00:47:14,398 --> 00:47:16,667
-The sauce is fantastic.
-It's the sauce.

1219
00:47:16,733 --> 00:47:18,368
Caviar's quite salty,

1220
00:47:18,435 --> 00:47:20,737
<i>so it pairs well</i>
<i>with sour notes in boiled wine.</i>

1221
00:47:20,804 --> 00:47:24,141
-I see.
-It contains a strong sour flavor.

1222
00:47:24,208 --> 00:47:26,343
He was after that.

1223
00:47:26,410 --> 00:47:28,946
The pie also looked like

1224
00:47:29,012 --> 00:47:31,715
he had just made it with some eggs, but…

1225
00:47:31,982 --> 00:47:32,816
YUM

1226
00:47:32,883 --> 00:47:34,117
But the texture

1227
00:47:34,184 --> 00:47:38,522
-reminds me of airy chestnuts.
-Is it fluffy?

1228
00:47:38,589 --> 00:47:42,526
<i>The dough gets chewy when made with water,</i>
<i>but it crumbles when you use butter.</i>

1229
00:47:42,593 --> 00:47:45,696
The sourness of the sauce and the caviar

1230
00:47:45,762 --> 00:47:47,631
creates such a nice balance.

1231
00:47:47,698 --> 00:47:49,566
-It's fantastic.
-Also,

1232
00:47:49,633 --> 00:47:53,437
-He eats well.
-…imagine how your wife would react.

1233
00:47:53,503 --> 00:47:56,306
She'll love this too.

1234
00:47:56,373 --> 00:48:00,277
But I'm wondering,
"Can I really make this?"

1235
00:48:00,344 --> 00:48:02,246
Is the sauce really that easy to make?

1236
00:48:02,312 --> 00:48:06,183
Even chefs fail often
if you add butter straight into wine.

1237
00:48:06,250 --> 00:48:08,385
<i>But first, cook butter with some flour,</i>

1238
00:48:08,452 --> 00:48:10,988
and then add some wine,
then it'll never be separated.

1239
00:48:11,054 --> 00:48:13,123
Literally anyone can make it.

1240
00:48:13,190 --> 00:48:14,524
Anyone?

1241
00:48:14,591 --> 00:48:16,526
Even you can do it, Sang-uk.

1242
00:48:16,593 --> 00:48:18,528
-He's quite surprised.
-That was so good.

1243
00:48:18,595 --> 00:48:20,564
Next, Chef Sung-jun's dish.

1244
00:48:20,631 --> 00:48:22,499
I Chicken You, Cha Jang-geum, My Love.

1245
00:48:22,933 --> 00:48:26,436
BABY HYUN-SUK'S
BETTER-THAN-MY-MASTER SAMGYETANG

1246
00:48:27,337 --> 00:48:30,507
WITH THE BEST PASTA OF SANG-UK'S LIFE

1247
00:48:31,375 --> 00:48:35,612
CHEF KWON SUNG-JUN'S
I CHICKEN YOU, CHA JANG-GEUM, MY LOVE

1248
00:48:35,679 --> 00:48:39,016
First of all, I expected to finish later,

1249
00:48:39,082 --> 00:48:41,551
so I added more sauce
to make it perfect to eat now.

1250
00:48:41,618 --> 00:48:44,154
-You planned it.
-Just toss it lightly before you eat it.

1251
00:48:44,221 --> 00:48:45,722
He even calculated that time.

1252
00:48:46,657 --> 00:48:48,926
It literally has nothing in it.

1253
00:48:48,992 --> 00:48:51,795
-Does it look like the one you had?
-Is it good?

1254
00:48:51,862 --> 00:48:53,497
The noodles are a little thinner,

1255
00:48:53,563 --> 00:48:56,099
but other than that, it looks the same.

1256
00:48:56,166 --> 00:48:57,968
CURIOUS

1257
00:48:58,035 --> 00:49:01,338
<i>-You only used salt and oil, right?</i>
<i>-Yes, just salt and oil.</i>

1258
00:49:01,405 --> 00:49:03,273
It'd be amazing
if this turns out to be good.

1259
00:49:03,340 --> 00:49:05,375
-He'd surpass that pasta.
-Yes.

1260
00:49:06,043 --> 00:49:07,644
-I'm nervous.
-It could've had something.

1261
00:49:07,711 --> 00:49:09,780
Will he get another taste
of the best pasta of his life?

1262
00:49:11,381 --> 00:49:13,717
DID HE RECREATE
THE BEST PASTA OF SANG-UK'S LIFE?

1263
00:49:15,419 --> 00:49:17,521
-His face says it all.
-It does.

1264
00:49:17,754 --> 00:49:19,456
OH, NO

1265
00:49:19,523 --> 00:49:20,691
You saw him?

1266
00:49:20,757 --> 00:49:21,758
-It's over?
-That's not it.

1267
00:49:21,825 --> 00:49:23,560
AWKWARD LAUGHTER

1268
00:49:23,627 --> 00:49:25,495
He hasn't even reached
the Milanese cleaner's.

1269
00:49:26,663 --> 00:49:27,831
Is it different in Naples?

1270
00:49:27,898 --> 00:49:32,703
It was rather late at night, so…

1271
00:49:32,769 --> 00:49:36,073
-You were hungry.
-Yes, I got there while I was hungry.

1272
00:49:36,139 --> 00:49:37,674
STARVATION PLAYED A ROLE

1273
00:49:38,141 --> 00:49:41,111
-It probably had other ingredients.
-They probably used stock.

1274
00:49:41,178 --> 00:49:44,014
-They could've removed the garlic pieces.
-It must've been seasoned.

1275
00:49:44,081 --> 00:49:46,750
They must've added some flavor with broth

1276
00:49:46,817 --> 00:49:49,453
or a tiny bit of cheese.

1277
00:49:49,519 --> 00:49:51,688
WHAT WAS THAT PASTA MADE OF?

1278
00:49:51,755 --> 00:49:53,590
May I move on?

1279
00:49:53,857 --> 00:49:55,625
ANXIOUS

1280
00:49:55,692 --> 00:49:58,962
<i>Samgyetang,</i> chicken soup,
is the main dish.

1281
00:49:59,696 --> 00:50:02,299
Cut it up and eat it with the broth.

1282
00:50:02,366 --> 00:50:03,433
When I tried the broth

1283
00:50:04,001 --> 00:50:06,903
with three minutes left in time,

1284
00:50:06,970 --> 00:50:09,573
-it was extraordinary.
-Did it have depth?

1285
00:50:09,639 --> 00:50:11,842
<i>-It was already finished.</i>
<i>-The soup?</i>

1286
00:50:11,908 --> 00:50:14,644
Then he added texture
with deep-fried enoki.

1287
00:50:14,711 --> 00:50:17,114
Why do I feel nervous?

1288
00:50:17,180 --> 00:50:19,149
Try it as you think of your wife.

1289
00:50:19,483 --> 00:50:20,317
YUM

1290
00:50:20,384 --> 00:50:21,518
I Chicken You.

1291
00:50:21,585 --> 00:50:23,854
I hope Cha Jang-geum will like that.

1292
00:50:23,920 --> 00:50:25,722
WILL HE MAKE A COMEBACK?

1293
00:50:25,789 --> 00:50:27,524
-It's…
-Does it taste like <i>samgyetang?</i>

1294
00:50:29,092 --> 00:50:30,727
It has lots of different flavors.

1295
00:50:30,794 --> 00:50:31,962
<i>Samgyetang,</i>

1296
00:50:32,029 --> 00:50:32,929
fried chicken…

1297
00:50:32,996 --> 00:50:34,998
He had it deep-fried.

1298
00:50:35,065 --> 00:50:36,833
It's so new.

1299
00:50:36,900 --> 00:50:39,002
At first, it tastes like <i>samgyetang.</i>

1300
00:50:39,069 --> 00:50:42,706
-Then it tastes more like fried chicken.
-It's been deep-fried.

1301
00:50:42,773 --> 00:50:45,675
-The soup is really good. And unique.
-Isn't it good?

1302
00:50:45,742 --> 00:50:48,078
It contains many different flavors.

1303
00:50:48,145 --> 00:50:49,546
This pasta
would taste so good with that broth.

1304
00:50:49,613 --> 00:50:52,449
-I actually wanted to suggest that.
-What?

1305
00:50:52,516 --> 00:50:55,685
I wanted him to try
adding the pasta into the soup.

1306
00:50:55,752 --> 00:50:57,587
-Pour the soup on top.
-The soup.

1307
00:50:57,654 --> 00:50:58,655
I see.

1308
00:50:58,722 --> 00:51:01,358
-That was the plan.
-Please see if it was like that.

1309
00:51:01,425 --> 00:51:02,959
Do I pour this over the pasta?

1310
00:51:03,026 --> 00:51:05,028
The broth was made with butter and cheese,

1311
00:51:05,095 --> 00:51:08,198
and I'm sure what he had
was butter and cheese pasta.

1312
00:51:08,265 --> 00:51:10,200
No one here has seen that pasta.

1313
00:51:10,267 --> 00:51:12,369
No. So I got suspicious.

1314
00:51:12,436 --> 00:51:15,272
Will it taste
like the pasta he had in Milan?

1315
00:51:15,338 --> 00:51:17,074
The best pasta he's ever had.

1316
00:51:20,177 --> 00:51:21,278
No.

1317
00:51:22,612 --> 00:51:24,948
-Don't jump to conclusions.
-That's too quick.

1318
00:51:25,015 --> 00:51:28,051
-Give him a moment.
-That was way too quick.

1319
00:51:28,118 --> 00:51:30,687
Give him time.
He hasn't even swallowed it.

1320
00:51:30,754 --> 00:51:32,689
Don't you get him already?
He would've said so.

1321
00:51:32,756 --> 00:51:34,791
IN A CRISIS

1322
00:51:34,858 --> 00:51:38,628
-Well, it is different from that one.
-It's different.

1323
00:51:38,695 --> 00:51:40,797
But it must be more flavorful now.

1324
00:51:40,864 --> 00:51:42,599
-Much better, right?
-Yes.

1325
00:51:42,666 --> 00:51:44,234
But it's not the same pasta.

1326
00:51:44,301 --> 00:51:46,770
It doesn't taste like that.

1327
00:51:46,837 --> 00:51:48,305
-It's different.
-It's not that one.

1328
00:51:48,371 --> 00:51:49,673
-Miss.
-He's from Naples.

1329
00:51:49,739 --> 00:51:51,508
PUSHING AWAY

1330
00:51:51,575 --> 00:51:52,809
He pushes it away

1331
00:51:52,876 --> 00:51:54,678
and goes back to <i>samgyetang.</i>

1332
00:51:54,744 --> 00:51:56,480
You wasted the soup for nothing.

1333
00:51:57,414 --> 00:52:00,016
-And it was so good.
-The soup is all gone.

1334
00:52:00,083 --> 00:52:02,285
He has no soup left.

1335
00:52:02,886 --> 00:52:04,187
The noodles absorbed it.

1336
00:52:04,421 --> 00:52:06,490
THE DETECTIVE'S EXPERIMENT FAILS

1337
00:52:06,556 --> 00:52:09,059
-You can't seem to hide your feelings.
-No.

1338
00:52:09,126 --> 00:52:10,894
But this one's good.

1339
00:52:10,961 --> 00:52:12,996
Did I sound too excited
for the first dish?

1340
00:52:13,063 --> 00:52:16,133
FINALLY REALIZES THE DIFFERENCE

1341
00:52:16,199 --> 00:52:18,001
I'm making it seem bad,
but that's not it.

1342
00:52:18,068 --> 00:52:19,936
-No?
-It's so good.

1343
00:52:20,003 --> 00:52:21,705
Jung-an will try some too,

1344
00:52:21,771 --> 00:52:23,607
so take your time
and continue to work on it

1345
00:52:23,673 --> 00:52:26,409
-before you decide.
-Okay.

1346
00:52:26,476 --> 00:52:28,478
HYUN-SUK'S DISH FIRST

1347
00:52:28,545 --> 00:52:31,381
Beef Wellington
with caviar beurre blanc sauce.

1348
00:52:31,448 --> 00:52:33,049
Thank you.

1349
00:52:33,116 --> 00:52:36,286
-Cooking, it's that easy.
-They're all for Hyun-suk now.

1350
00:52:36,353 --> 00:52:38,021
He's cool when he doesn't talk.

1351
00:52:38,088 --> 00:52:39,856
-"It's that easy."
-But he talks way too much.

1352
00:52:39,923 --> 00:52:41,658
-Our Chef Hyun-suk.
-Okay.

1353
00:52:41,725 --> 00:52:43,894
-Use the spoon to get the sauce.
-With the sauce.

1354
00:52:44,461 --> 00:52:45,295
Okay.

1355
00:52:45,362 --> 00:52:47,130
CAREFULLY TAKES A BITE

1356
00:52:47,197 --> 00:52:49,566
-She'll be good at reviewing.
-She's an honest one.

1357
00:52:50,200 --> 00:52:51,034
What's it like?

1358
00:52:51,635 --> 00:52:52,969
Among what I've recently eaten,

1359
00:52:53,537 --> 00:52:55,939
it's the best properly cooked dish
I have had.

1360
00:52:56,706 --> 00:52:58,575
-I mean it. It's that good.
-Recently?

1361
00:52:58,942 --> 00:53:00,443
GASP, MMM

1362
00:53:00,510 --> 00:53:02,712
-What is it?
-This sauce is really good.

1363
00:53:02,779 --> 00:53:05,849
The sourness brings out your appetite.

1364
00:53:05,916 --> 00:53:09,286
When I had the sauce after the meat,
I could go back for more.

1365
00:53:09,352 --> 00:53:11,821
-I see.
-It's excellent.

1366
00:53:11,888 --> 00:53:14,291
Next, Chef Sung-jun's dish.

1367
00:53:14,357 --> 00:53:17,294
Sung-jun does not bring his pasta.

1368
00:53:17,360 --> 00:53:18,795
Just the chicken.

1369
00:53:18,862 --> 00:53:20,997
-Just the <i>samgyetang.</i>
-Thank you.

1370
00:53:23,867 --> 00:53:25,235
I'll try the soup first.

1371
00:53:26,503 --> 00:53:27,537
The chicken soup.

1372
00:53:27,604 --> 00:53:28,939
SLURP

1373
00:53:30,473 --> 00:53:31,575
STARING…

1374
00:53:31,641 --> 00:53:32,842
She looks at him.

1375
00:53:32,909 --> 00:53:35,645
She stares at him softly.

1376
00:53:35,712 --> 00:53:37,514
"Who is this chef?"

1377
00:53:37,581 --> 00:53:39,783
Never judge a book by its cover.

1378
00:53:41,451 --> 00:53:42,552
What did the cover look like?

1379
00:53:42,619 --> 00:53:44,454
-Was it crude?
-What did you think of his "cover"?

1380
00:53:44,521 --> 00:53:46,323
WHAT'S SO BAD ABOUT SUNG-JUN?

1381
00:53:46,389 --> 00:53:49,793
-He has that sleazy look.
-Sleazy?

1382
00:53:49,859 --> 00:53:52,229
-You look sleazy.
-But his food isn't like that.

1383
00:53:52,295 --> 00:53:55,498
-Sleazy and tacky.
-She thought it'd taste sleazy like him,

1384
00:53:55,565 --> 00:53:57,033
-…but it had depth.
-It does.

1385
00:53:57,100 --> 00:53:58,735
-Gosh.
-"Gosh."

1386
00:53:58,802 --> 00:54:01,071
And how does it end
with a refreshing flavor?

1387
00:54:01,137 --> 00:54:03,873
It has a refreshing finish.

1388
00:54:03,940 --> 00:54:06,543
Jung-an seems to like this one better.

1389
00:54:07,344 --> 00:54:10,780
-It's different from ordinary <i>samgyetang.</i>
-It is.

1390
00:54:10,847 --> 00:54:14,184
It feels like a nutritious dish too.

1391
00:54:14,251 --> 00:54:15,118
It makes you healthier.

1392
00:54:15,185 --> 00:54:17,587
It really warms you up,

1393
00:54:17,654 --> 00:54:20,390
and it's almost comforting.

1394
00:54:20,457 --> 00:54:21,691
Have I been too lonely?

1395
00:54:23,159 --> 00:54:25,695
I must've been feeling empty inside.

1396
00:54:25,762 --> 00:54:29,332
<i>But this dish filled me up</i>
<i>with its warmth.</i>

1397
00:54:29,399 --> 00:54:32,302
It's time for Sang-uk to decide

1398
00:54:32,369 --> 00:54:33,870
based on the theme.

1399
00:54:33,937 --> 00:54:35,705
<i>Press one for Sung-jun</i>

1400
00:54:36,339 --> 00:54:37,674
<i>and two for Hyun-suk.</i>

1401
00:54:38,775 --> 00:54:41,478
Make your decision and press the button.

1402
00:54:42,178 --> 00:54:44,581
Press one for Sung-jun, two for Hyun-suk.

1403
00:54:44,648 --> 00:54:45,515
-Did you do it?
-Yes.

1404
00:54:45,582 --> 00:54:47,884
-That was quick.
-All right.

1405
00:54:47,951 --> 00:54:49,653
He chose very calmly.

1406
00:54:49,719 --> 00:54:53,390
And he just smiled at Hyun-suk.

1407
00:54:53,456 --> 00:54:55,392
Does he feel bad?

1408
00:54:55,458 --> 00:54:56,926
Let's find out already.

1409
00:54:56,993 --> 00:55:00,563
Let's find out the result. Show us.

1410
00:55:00,630 --> 00:55:02,265
They're one star apart.

1411
00:55:02,332 --> 00:55:03,933
Hyun-suk has ten wins,

1412
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:05,535
and Sung-jun has nine.

1413
00:55:05,602 --> 00:55:07,070
A dish for Sang-uk's wife.

1414
00:55:07,137 --> 00:55:10,373
Will it be Hyun-suk or Sung-jun?

1415
00:55:10,440 --> 00:55:14,477
Which side did Sang-uk choose?

1416
00:55:14,544 --> 00:55:16,546
-Who's the winner?
-Who did he choose?

1417
00:55:19,082 --> 00:55:20,617
-Who's the winner?
-Who did he choose?

1418
00:55:20,850 --> 00:55:22,118
CHOI HYUN-SUK WINS

1419
00:55:22,185 --> 00:55:23,219
It's Choi Hyun-suk.

1420
00:55:23,620 --> 00:55:25,622
CHEF CHOI HYUN-SUK'S CHOI-SBAND WINS

1421
00:55:25,689 --> 00:55:27,791
Hyun-suk wins.

1422
00:55:27,857 --> 00:55:29,693
SWOOSH

1423
00:55:29,759 --> 00:55:31,294
-I'm sorry.
-A new lesson for Sung-jun.

1424
00:55:31,594 --> 00:55:32,962
HYUN-SUK TEACHES HIM A LESSON

1425
00:55:33,029 --> 00:55:34,464
"This is cooking."

1426
00:55:34,531 --> 00:55:36,800
The elderly Sung-jun wins.

1427
00:55:36,866 --> 00:55:39,202
-The elderly Sung-jun?
-The elderly Sung-jun wins.

1428
00:55:39,269 --> 00:55:41,404
-Hyun-suk did it.
-Don't take it the wrong way.

1429
00:55:41,471 --> 00:55:45,275
It wasn't that this dish wasn't good.

1430
00:55:45,342 --> 00:55:47,644
Both dishes were great,

1431
00:55:47,711 --> 00:55:50,313
<i>but this caviar sauce</i>

1432
00:55:50,380 --> 00:55:52,716
<i>had the kind of flavor I've never had.</i>

1433
00:55:52,782 --> 00:55:57,620
I thought it'd be nice
if my wife could try the same thing,

1434
00:55:57,687 --> 00:56:00,023
-so I chose him.
-It's a glamorous temptation.

1435
00:56:00,857 --> 00:56:02,592
The pasta did it, right?

1436
00:56:02,659 --> 00:56:04,728
The one I had in Milan wasn't like this.

1437
00:56:04,794 --> 00:56:07,430
Maybe I should've described it better.

1438
00:56:07,497 --> 00:56:09,299
-But it wasn't this.
-I'm sure it wasn't just salt.

1439
00:56:09,366 --> 00:56:12,068
Please put a star on Chef Hyun-suk.

1440
00:56:12,969 --> 00:56:16,406
-This means…
-He's alone in second place.

1441
00:56:16,473 --> 00:56:19,042
Congratulations, Hyun-suk.

1442
00:56:19,109 --> 00:56:19,976
Congrats!

1443
00:56:21,544 --> 00:56:25,482
<i>Let us begin the second cook-off</i>
<i>using Sang-uk's ingredients.</i>

1444
00:56:25,548 --> 00:56:28,785
Canine villain, Chef Sam Kim,

1445
00:56:28,852 --> 00:56:32,789
versus fiery rice paddle of Yeonhui-dong,
Chef Jung Ho-young.

1446
00:56:32,856 --> 00:56:33,957
Fiery rice paddle?

1447
00:56:34,023 --> 00:56:35,825
-Not the fiery fists.
-No, rice paddle.

1448
00:56:35,892 --> 00:56:36,960
Nice nickname.

1449
00:56:37,026 --> 00:56:40,764
The two once dreamed
of winning the Best Couple Award,

1450
00:56:40,830 --> 00:56:44,434
but their friendship
slowly began to fall apart.

1451
00:56:44,501 --> 00:56:46,903
I've even heard this rumor.

1452
00:56:46,970 --> 00:56:50,173
They pretended to be close
to land an ad gig,

1453
00:56:50,240 --> 00:56:52,275
but when nothing happened,

1454
00:56:52,342 --> 00:56:55,278
they began to draw the line.

1455
00:56:55,345 --> 00:57:00,083
Recently, we received an ad offer

1456
00:57:00,150 --> 00:57:01,818
from a big corporation.

1457
00:57:01,885 --> 00:57:04,921
An ad offer from a big corporation
for both of you?

1458
00:57:04,988 --> 00:57:08,324
I said I could do it with Ho-young,

1459
00:57:08,391 --> 00:57:11,294
but I later heard
that he said he'd consider it.

1460
00:57:11,361 --> 00:57:12,695
Me?

1461
00:57:12,762 --> 00:57:14,964
-It was Ho-young?
-Yes.

1462
00:57:15,031 --> 00:57:17,033
-Why not?
-For a big corporation?

1463
00:57:17,100 --> 00:57:18,701
Why wouldn't he do it?

1464
00:57:18,768 --> 00:57:20,336
It was probably a good gig.

1465
00:57:20,403 --> 00:57:22,505
Well, I wanted to work it out too.

1466
00:57:22,572 --> 00:57:25,074
But Sam was to get paid much more than me.

1467
00:57:25,475 --> 00:57:27,977
THE NEGOTIATION COLLAPSED(?)

1468
00:57:28,044 --> 00:57:30,013
I mean, I wanted it to be similar,

1469
00:57:30,079 --> 00:57:31,981
but because of his recent popularity,

1470
00:57:32,048 --> 00:57:34,751
he asked for more,
and I was to receive the rest.

1471
00:57:34,818 --> 00:57:36,019
The rest?

1472
00:57:36,085 --> 00:57:37,587
The remainder.

1473
00:57:37,654 --> 00:57:40,356
So you two were supposed to split
whatever amount you got.

1474
00:57:40,423 --> 00:57:43,726
He was like, "If I'm only getting that,
we're both not getting any."

1475
00:57:44,027 --> 00:57:45,161
RUINS IT FOR EVERYONE(?)

1476
00:57:45,228 --> 00:57:47,564
-That's why you declined it.
-That's true.

1477
00:57:47,630 --> 00:57:49,799
I would've understood if it were 6 to 4,

1478
00:57:49,866 --> 00:57:51,167
-but it was 7 to 3.
-That's a lot.

1479
00:57:51,234 --> 00:57:52,669
The difference is huge.

1480
00:57:52,735 --> 00:57:56,005
-Call me. I'll do it for three.
-It's a sensitive topic.

1481
00:57:56,072 --> 00:57:57,106
So it's off?

1482
00:57:57,173 --> 00:57:59,008
I haven't heard back after that.

1483
00:57:59,876 --> 00:58:01,478
Let's call them.

1484
00:58:01,544 --> 00:58:03,580
-Let's steal it.
-We'll call them.

1485
00:58:03,646 --> 00:58:06,883
Do you guys have no secrets?
Do you share everything?

1486
00:58:06,950 --> 00:58:09,285
We say just about everything
in the green room.

1487
00:58:09,352 --> 00:58:11,354
We know everything
that's happening in their lives.

1488
00:58:11,421 --> 00:58:13,823
-It must be true.
-There must've been a big difference.

1489
00:58:13,890 --> 00:58:15,124
THEY'RE JUST JOKING

1490
00:58:15,191 --> 00:58:17,460
Sort things out for them, Sang-uk.

1491
00:58:18,094 --> 00:58:19,829
-Me?
-That's so random.

1492
00:58:19,896 --> 00:58:22,432
You have experience.
And you've had many ad gigs.

1493
00:58:22,499 --> 00:58:24,133
Wait, me?

1494
00:58:24,200 --> 00:58:28,171
Whenever Sam is in a cook-off,

1495
00:58:28,238 --> 00:58:29,472
something goes wrong.

1496
00:58:29,539 --> 00:58:32,141
-He got me.
-Things randomly happen.

1497
00:58:32,208 --> 00:58:34,244
First, the missing bolt.

1498
00:58:34,310 --> 00:58:36,145
-The oven's power.
-The oven went off.

1499
00:58:36,212 --> 00:58:38,948
-Jong-won's dishcloth got burnt.
-That too.

1500
00:58:39,015 --> 00:58:41,217
There's no evidence. What did I do?

1501
00:58:41,284 --> 00:58:42,585
He's acting suspicious.

1502
00:58:42,652 --> 00:58:44,254
-Whoa.
-"There's no evidence."

1503
00:58:44,320 --> 00:58:46,689
-Why is he snappy?
-I have been

1504
00:58:46,756 --> 00:58:49,726
a part of a true crime show
for a long time.

1505
00:58:49,792 --> 00:58:53,763
Anyone who says that is 100% the culprit.

1506
00:58:53,830 --> 00:58:55,098
-"Bring evidence."
-"Bring evidence."

1507
00:58:55,164 --> 00:58:57,300
-That's the first thing they say.
-"Do you have evidence?"

1508
00:58:57,367 --> 00:58:58,801
"Do you have evidence?"

1509
00:58:58,868 --> 00:59:02,171
And with enough circumstantial evidence,
you will find something decisive.

1510
00:59:02,238 --> 00:59:05,575
Even in dramas and films,
it wouldn't be fun to have

1511
00:59:05,642 --> 00:59:08,278
-the villain look like a villain.
-True.

1512
00:59:08,344 --> 00:59:12,215
<i>He's been nothing but a saint</i>
<i>since the beginning of the show.</i>

1513
00:59:12,282 --> 00:59:14,083
So what if there was a twist?

1514
00:59:14,150 --> 00:59:16,286
He's such a kind-hearted man, though.

1515
00:59:16,352 --> 00:59:19,122
-Odd things happen without evidence.
-It works.

1516
00:59:19,188 --> 00:59:20,290
-Is the oven okay?
-Is it?

1517
00:59:20,356 --> 00:59:22,125
Check everything you need to.

1518
00:59:22,191 --> 00:59:24,460
-The freezer.
-Check what?

1519
00:59:24,527 --> 00:59:25,995
Check the fridge too.

1520
00:59:26,062 --> 00:59:28,831
-Your knife and ingredients too.
-You have to check.

1521
00:59:28,898 --> 00:59:30,500
-How about the cameras?
-The bolt too.

1522
00:59:30,567 --> 00:59:32,468
-How about the solo shots?
-The blender.

1523
00:59:32,535 --> 00:59:34,871
Are you all siding against me?

1524
00:59:35,271 --> 00:59:37,307
HUH? HE SNAPPED AGAIN

1525
00:59:37,373 --> 00:59:39,709
When they get cornered,
they will try to bite.

1526
00:59:39,776 --> 00:59:41,411
The culprit, that is.

1527
00:59:41,477 --> 00:59:43,346
I kept this on before. Who turned it off?

1528
00:59:43,413 --> 00:59:45,515
CAUGHT RED-HANDED(?)

1529
00:59:45,582 --> 00:59:47,550
The loser gets a penalty, right?

1530
00:59:47,617 --> 00:59:49,586
Yes, we're boiling potatoes back there.

1531
00:59:49,652 --> 00:59:52,255
Yes, we have to boil them.

1532
00:59:52,322 --> 00:59:55,558
The moment we see the result,
the potato goes into your mouth.

1533
00:59:55,625 --> 00:59:57,360
I mean, it's great.

1534
00:59:57,427 --> 00:59:59,128
-He can lose his canines.
-Watch your teeth.

1535
00:59:59,495 --> 01:00:00,330
WILL HE LOSE HIS CANINES(?)

1536
01:00:00,396 --> 01:00:02,765
-Will they fall out?
-He may lose his canines.

1537
01:00:02,832 --> 01:00:05,969
They've prepared a perfect potato
shaped like this mouth.

1538
01:00:06,035 --> 01:00:07,203
-Steaming hot.
-Like this.

1539
01:00:08,037 --> 01:00:09,872
CHEF HO-YOUNG'S INGREDIENTS:
THIN SKIRT, THIN PORK BELLY,

1540
01:00:09,939 --> 01:00:12,208
SEA URCHIN ROE, PACIFIC WHITE SHRIMP,
CILANTRO, NAPA CABBAGE, AND MORE

1541
01:00:12,275 --> 01:00:14,010
What are you making, Ho-young?

1542
01:00:14,077 --> 01:00:16,946
Here's the name of my dish.

1543
01:00:17,647 --> 01:00:20,750
Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>

1544
01:00:20,817 --> 01:00:23,486
-Nagging Sam Kim.
-Is it Nagasaki <i>champon?</i>

1545
01:00:23,553 --> 01:00:25,722
-Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>
-Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>

1546
01:00:25,788 --> 01:00:28,224
THE CHAIRMAN LOVES IT

1547
01:00:28,291 --> 01:00:30,193
He said he liked Japanese food,

1548
01:00:30,259 --> 01:00:32,161
so I'll make Nagasaki <i>champon</i>

1549
01:00:32,228 --> 01:00:35,064
to go with a drink of soju.

1550
01:00:35,131 --> 01:00:36,766
He also said he liked thin skirt,

1551
01:00:36,833 --> 01:00:40,336
so I'll season it, cook it on a gridiron,

1552
01:00:40,403 --> 01:00:42,639
and mix it with cilantro
in sweet and sour sauce.

1553
01:00:42,705 --> 01:00:45,408
I'll serve that along with the noodles.

1554
01:00:45,475 --> 01:00:48,778
On top of the thin skirt,
I'll pour sea urchin and egg yolk sauce.

1555
01:00:48,845 --> 01:00:49,979
AMAZED

1556
01:00:50,046 --> 01:00:51,781
-A Japanese chef's sea urchin roe.
-So those.

1557
01:00:51,848 --> 01:00:52,915
The moment you eat it…

1558
01:00:52,982 --> 01:00:54,083
GULP

1559
01:00:54,150 --> 01:00:58,721
…I'll make sure you start craving soju.

1560
01:00:58,788 --> 01:00:59,856
He's a true chef.

1561
01:00:59,922 --> 01:01:02,959
He just gulped
as he talked about his own dish.

1562
01:01:03,026 --> 01:01:06,863
-He's the one who gulped.
-No other chef does that.

1563
01:01:06,929 --> 01:01:08,931
-I was craving a drink too.
-Of course.

1564
01:01:08,998 --> 01:01:10,533
That's a rare trait.

1565
01:01:11,367 --> 01:01:13,369
CHEF SAM'S INGREDIENTS:
STRIP LOIN, BONELESS CHICKEN THIGH,

1566
01:01:13,436 --> 01:01:15,538
SEASONED EEL, SEA URCHIN ROE,
NAPA CABBAGE, BOK CHOY, AND MORE

1567
01:01:15,605 --> 01:01:17,373
What are you making, Sam?

1568
01:01:17,440 --> 01:01:18,908
I prepared a dish called

1569
01:01:19,475 --> 01:01:20,543
Jung Ho-young!

1570
01:01:20,610 --> 01:01:22,278
You Sukiyaki!

1571
01:01:23,112 --> 01:01:26,115
Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!

1572
01:01:27,550 --> 01:01:29,852
-Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!
-Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!

1573
01:01:29,919 --> 01:01:32,789
-Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!
-Sukiyaki.

1574
01:01:33,322 --> 01:01:34,557
HURT…

1575
01:01:34,624 --> 01:01:37,894
Are you making this
to call him "<i>sukiya,</i>" like "punk"?

1576
01:01:37,960 --> 01:01:40,897
Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!
What's it like?

1577
01:01:40,963 --> 01:01:44,200
I'm making sukiyaki,

1578
01:01:44,267 --> 01:01:46,903
with a side of skewers.

1579
01:01:46,969 --> 01:01:48,171
And for the next one,

1580
01:01:48,237 --> 01:01:51,174
I prepared a cold noodle dish
using sea urchin roe.

1581
01:01:51,240 --> 01:01:54,177
Because he has played a chairman,

1582
01:01:54,243 --> 01:01:58,614
I thought a more classy soju snack
would be more fitting.

1583
01:02:00,650 --> 01:02:02,385
Sam's making Japanese food too.

1584
01:02:02,452 --> 01:02:04,020
-Yes.
-It's Japanese cuisine.

1585
01:02:04,087 --> 01:02:04,921
Both of them.

1586
01:02:04,987 --> 01:02:06,255
What's it called again?

1587
01:02:06,322 --> 01:02:09,792
Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!

1588
01:02:09,859 --> 01:02:12,361
-That works nicely.
-It does.

1589
01:02:12,428 --> 01:02:14,897
Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>

1590
01:02:14,964 --> 01:02:17,100
-That was good. Sukiyaki. Okay.
-Sukiyaki.

1591
01:02:17,166 --> 01:02:18,868
Now, let us begin

1592
01:02:18,935 --> 01:02:22,105
the cook-off for Sang-uk,
Chairman Booze, Time for Some Soju.

1593
01:02:22,171 --> 01:02:25,108
Here to pull out both of Sam's canines,

1594
01:02:25,174 --> 01:02:27,543
the fiery rice paddle
of Yeonhui-dong, Chef Jung Ho-young.

1595
01:02:27,610 --> 01:02:30,313
He's ready to bite
even without his canines,

1596
01:02:30,379 --> 01:02:33,416
the biggest villain of the show,
Chef Sam Kim.

1597
01:02:33,483 --> 01:02:36,018
Which one of them will satisfy
Chairman Joo's appetite?

1598
01:02:36,085 --> 01:02:39,422
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1599
01:02:40,723 --> 01:02:42,759
A DISH TO MAKE CHAIRMAN BOOZE CRAVE SOJU

1600
01:02:42,825 --> 01:02:45,294
Make sure the chairman is pleased.

1601
01:02:45,361 --> 01:02:48,197
He must automatically crave soju
the moment he sees your dish.

1602
01:02:48,264 --> 01:02:50,566
He picked up soy sauce
to make the sauce first.

1603
01:02:50,633 --> 01:02:53,069
Why are you making that?

1604
01:02:53,136 --> 01:02:55,371
It's for the thin skirt.

1605
01:02:55,438 --> 01:02:57,140
-That looks good.
-Isn't it <i>champon?</i>

1606
01:02:57,206 --> 01:02:59,342
A dish perfect for soju.

1607
01:02:59,408 --> 01:03:00,243
SALT

1608
01:03:00,309 --> 01:03:03,179
<i>Suki</i> is actually "spade" in Japanese.

1609
01:03:03,246 --> 01:03:04,814
-That's <i>suki.</i>
-I see.

1610
01:03:04,881 --> 01:03:08,718
Apparently, people used that
to grill and eat meat,

1611
01:03:08,785 --> 01:03:10,920
hence, the name sukiyaki.

1612
01:03:10,987 --> 01:03:14,423
Are you talking about the spade
as in the farming tool?

1613
01:03:14,490 --> 01:03:16,893
-That's what <i>suki</i> means?
-Yes.

1614
01:03:16,959 --> 01:03:19,695
-How does he know so much?
-He does.

1615
01:03:19,762 --> 01:03:21,364
He's a walking encyclopedia.

1616
01:03:21,931 --> 01:03:22,899
GRABS THE SHRIMP BAIT(?)

1617
01:03:22,965 --> 01:03:25,468
-The shrimp used for bait.
-The bait.

1618
01:03:25,535 --> 01:03:28,471
It may be bait,
but it's fresh from Noryangjin.

1619
01:03:28,538 --> 01:03:30,273
So there's no problem with them.

1620
01:03:30,339 --> 01:03:31,707
Those look meaty.

1621
01:03:31,774 --> 01:03:36,512
Sam usually sat in front of Ho-young
and peeled those for him.

1622
01:03:37,346 --> 01:03:39,248
CHEF SOMEONE ALWAYS SAT THERE AND HELPED

1623
01:03:39,782 --> 01:03:43,519
THEY WERE ABOUT TO BECOME THE BEST COUPLE

1624
01:03:43,853 --> 01:03:46,155
BUT NOW, THEY'RE FAR(?) APART

1625
01:03:46,222 --> 01:03:47,857
It'd be nice if someone peeled these.

1626
01:03:47,924 --> 01:03:49,826
THE SOMEONE DOESN'T RESPOND

1627
01:03:49,892 --> 01:03:52,695
-Your fan gave you really meaty ones.
-Yes.

1628
01:03:52,762 --> 01:03:54,564
The shrimp are so meaty,

1629
01:03:54,630 --> 01:03:56,199
I can't bait-lieve they're bait.

1630
01:03:56,465 --> 01:03:58,568
JUNG-HWAN?

1631
01:04:00,837 --> 01:04:02,839
Gosh, he's so funny.

1632
01:04:02,905 --> 01:04:04,774
This is what I call a joke.

1633
01:04:04,841 --> 01:04:07,276
-My gosh.
-He doesn't bother to look.

1634
01:04:07,743 --> 01:04:10,680
…QUIETLY TURNS IT ON

1635
01:04:10,746 --> 01:04:12,515
-He won't look.
-No.

1636
01:04:12,582 --> 01:04:14,617
I thought he was about to give us
some shrimp trivia.

1637
01:04:15,184 --> 01:04:16,886
That was so funny.

1638
01:04:17,253 --> 01:04:20,122
CHOKING UP(?)

1639
01:04:20,189 --> 01:04:22,925
You're wrapping shrimp with that?

1640
01:04:22,992 --> 01:04:25,528
I'll grill them like this
and use them as toppings.

1641
01:04:26,128 --> 01:04:28,664
Shrimp and pork taste great together.

1642
01:04:28,731 --> 01:04:31,000
Is that the pork belly
from the brand Sung-ju models for?

1643
01:04:31,067 --> 01:04:32,401
The one of great quality.

1644
01:04:32,468 --> 01:04:34,136
It's great quality.

1645
01:04:34,203 --> 01:04:37,006
He's using great ingredients,
shrimp, thin pork belly,

1646
01:04:37,073 --> 01:04:38,674
and thin skirt.

1647
01:04:38,908 --> 01:04:39,976
PREPPING BONELESS CHICKEN THIGHS

1648
01:04:40,042 --> 01:04:42,311
Why are you using eel for sukiyaki?

1649
01:04:42,378 --> 01:04:45,548
I'm just using the sauce
for the chicken skewer.

1650
01:04:45,615 --> 01:04:47,817
THE SAUCE FOR CHICKEN SKEWER

1651
01:04:47,884 --> 01:04:51,287
It's drenched in teriyaki sauce,
so he's just using that.

1652
01:04:51,754 --> 01:04:53,689
MOVE THE CHICKEN TO ANOTHER PAN

1653
01:04:55,024 --> 01:04:56,225
-Sam?
-Yes?

1654
01:04:56,292 --> 01:04:59,095
-What's that meat?
-This? Strip loin.

1655
01:04:59,161 --> 01:05:03,099
It's strip loin, but cut for sukiyaki.
So it's already thin.

1656
01:05:03,165 --> 01:05:05,368
Does the green onion play a role?

1657
01:05:05,434 --> 01:05:08,337
-It adds smokiness and sweetness.
-I see.

1658
01:05:08,404 --> 01:05:11,040
REMOVING STRIP LOIN
AFTER SEARING IT LIGHTLY

1659
01:05:11,307 --> 01:05:12,208
MAKING SUKIYAKI BROTH

1660
01:05:12,275 --> 01:05:14,944
-Sukiyaki is Japanese cuisine.
-It is.

1661
01:05:15,011 --> 01:05:18,648
1. MIX WATER, SOY SAUCE, COOKING WINE,
SUGAR, OYSTER SAUCE, AND TSUYU

1662
01:05:18,714 --> 01:05:21,751
He's using all three vegetables
perfect for sukiyaki.

1663
01:05:21,817 --> 01:05:24,353
Enoki, napa cabbage, and bok choy.

1664
01:05:24,420 --> 01:05:29,225
Ho-young and Sam
are cooking Japanese cuisine.

1665
01:05:29,292 --> 01:05:31,861
It would crush Ho-young's pride to lose.

1666
01:05:31,928 --> 01:05:33,429
-That's right.
-That's right.

1667
01:05:33,763 --> 01:05:35,631
NAGASAKI CHAMPON FOR HIS PRIDE

1668
01:05:35,965 --> 01:05:38,134
1. COOK GREEN ONION, MINCED GARLIC,
ONION, AND THIN PORK BELLY

1669
01:05:39,402 --> 01:05:41,370
ADDING SMOKY FLAVOR

1670
01:05:41,804 --> 01:05:43,806
-Do you also like <i>champon</i>?
-Yes.

1671
01:05:43,873 --> 01:05:46,309
-She looks excited too.
-I am.

1672
01:05:46,375 --> 01:05:48,544
-What's that?
-<i>Gomtang, </i>ox bone soup?

1673
01:05:48,611 --> 01:05:52,281
-It has to be good with that broth.
-That's just guaranteed to be good.

1674
01:05:52,682 --> 01:05:53,883
MSG

1675
01:05:57,153 --> 01:05:58,721
Did Sam seal this?

1676
01:05:58,788 --> 01:06:01,390
-It won't come out.
-It won't?

1677
01:06:01,857 --> 01:06:03,159
SCRAPING

1678
01:06:03,225 --> 01:06:04,060
It has hardened.

1679
01:06:04,126 --> 01:06:06,262
-How?
-This.

1680
01:06:06,529 --> 01:06:08,364
AS ALWAYS, ANOTHER MYSTERIOUS SITUATION

1681
01:06:08,864 --> 01:06:11,267
THROW IN ANOTHER COLD CASE

1682
01:06:11,334 --> 01:06:14,637
Mr. Chairman, what do you think
of the process so far?

1683
01:06:14,704 --> 01:06:18,441
They both seem like
they're calling for soju already.

1684
01:06:18,507 --> 01:06:21,677
However, there is just one thing.

1685
01:06:22,545 --> 01:06:24,013
It'd be nice to have

1686
01:06:24,080 --> 01:06:25,514
something a bit spicier.

1687
01:06:25,581 --> 01:06:28,551
-Make it spicy.
-Ho-young, pour red pepper powder in.

1688
01:06:28,617 --> 01:06:31,053
-He says it only looks sweet.
-Add the powder.

1689
01:06:31,120 --> 01:06:34,724
-Cheongyang pepper.
-Cheongyang peppers? Add a lot.

1690
01:06:34,790 --> 01:06:36,158
Spiciness.

1691
01:06:36,492 --> 01:06:38,527
NAPA CABBAGE

1692
01:06:38,594 --> 01:06:40,563
-Whoa.
-That's too much ground pepper.

1693
01:06:40,629 --> 01:06:42,264
-To make it spicy.
-Spicy.

1694
01:06:42,331 --> 01:06:44,667
It looks rather salty.

1695
01:06:44,734 --> 01:06:48,070
The recipes look somewhat similar.

1696
01:06:48,137 --> 01:06:50,039
Before this, he watched my channel

1697
01:06:50,106 --> 01:06:51,540
and asked for my recipe.

1698
01:06:51,607 --> 01:06:53,042
That's true.

1699
01:06:53,609 --> 01:06:55,311
He did look it up online.

1700
01:06:55,378 --> 01:06:57,346
-There was a sukiyaki recipe.
-That one?

1701
01:06:57,413 --> 01:07:00,049
MAKING SUKIYAKI SAUCE

1702
01:07:00,583 --> 01:07:02,485
EGG AND TRUFFLE CARPACCIO

1703
01:07:02,551 --> 01:07:06,222
Sam's scraping out the truffle paste.
I guess there isn't much left.

1704
01:07:06,288 --> 01:07:08,557
-Someone else used it earlier.
-Come on, Chef Hyun-suk.

1705
01:07:08,624 --> 01:07:10,126
Hyun-suk used too much.

1706
01:07:10,192 --> 01:07:12,094
This is the truffle paste you had.

1707
01:07:12,661 --> 01:07:14,163
Can you leave me a little?

1708
01:07:14,230 --> 01:07:17,066
My pride couldn't stand the idea
of making something less than perfect

1709
01:07:17,133 --> 01:07:20,202
just to be nice and share something.

1710
01:07:21,137 --> 01:07:21,971
So he used a lot.

1711
01:07:22,038 --> 01:07:24,106
He used as much as he wanted to.

1712
01:07:24,173 --> 01:07:25,541
No.

1713
01:07:25,608 --> 01:07:27,743
-As much as I needed to.
-As much as you needed.

1714
01:07:27,810 --> 01:07:29,879
He didn't go overboard.

1715
01:07:30,179 --> 01:07:31,881
SAM HAS A LONG WAY TO GO

1716
01:07:32,348 --> 01:07:34,083
GRILLS GREEN ONION AND ONION

1717
01:07:34,150 --> 01:07:36,986
-That's <i>yakitori,</i> chicken skewers.
-Chicken skewers.

1718
01:07:38,821 --> 01:07:41,023
GREEN ONION, CHICKEN

1719
01:07:41,090 --> 01:07:43,826
You have seven minutes left.

1720
01:07:43,893 --> 01:07:45,428
You're more than halfway done.

1721
01:07:46,629 --> 01:07:49,031
Corn noodles are extremely starchy,

1722
01:07:49,098 --> 01:07:51,100
so you must cook them quickly
like <i>naengmyeon.</i>

1723
01:07:51,867 --> 01:07:53,969
-Those are for the <i>champon?</i>
-Yes.

1724
01:07:54,370 --> 01:07:55,971
SAM KIM USES THIN WHEAT NOODLES

1725
01:07:56,038 --> 01:07:58,874
-Are those thin wheat noodles?
-That's it.

1726
01:07:59,208 --> 01:08:01,811
BOILING SUKIYAKI

1727
01:08:01,877 --> 01:08:03,479
It looks too salty.

1728
01:08:03,546 --> 01:08:05,781
-Does it? It seems too salty?
-The sukiyaki.

1729
01:08:05,848 --> 01:08:07,550
Sung-jun chimes in.

1730
01:08:07,616 --> 01:08:10,786
Did you see Sam glare at Sung-jun
the moment he said it looked salty?

1731
01:08:10,853 --> 01:08:13,722
<i>"Hey, you punk. What do you know?"</i>

1732
01:08:13,789 --> 01:08:15,724
-"You're next."
-Am I?

1733
01:08:15,791 --> 01:08:18,227
He glares at you
when you say something to attack him.

1734
01:08:18,294 --> 01:08:20,129
He won't be patient anymore.

1735
01:08:20,196 --> 01:08:23,399
And when he competes with someone
who rubbed him the wrong way,

1736
01:08:23,466 --> 01:08:24,600
something breaks.

1737
01:08:24,934 --> 01:08:27,403
SAM KIM WILL REMEMBER THAT

1738
01:08:28,737 --> 01:08:31,874
THE REMAINING VEGGIES
GET ADDED TO THE SUKIYAKI

1739
01:08:34,743 --> 01:08:35,611
Wait, what?

1740
01:08:36,245 --> 01:08:38,347
RELOCATING THE SAUCE

1741
01:08:38,414 --> 01:08:40,416
It should be the other way around.

1742
01:08:40,483 --> 01:08:42,918
-Something's odd.
-What is that?

1743
01:08:42,985 --> 01:08:44,920
-It's the other way around.
-What is that?

1744
01:08:44,987 --> 01:08:47,790
-What's he doing?
-The chairman may be in abundance,

1745
01:08:47,857 --> 01:08:50,593
but you're using just the sauce
and discarding the eel?

1746
01:08:50,659 --> 01:08:52,728
It looks like he needs a potato.

1747
01:08:54,096 --> 01:08:58,667
Do you have any idea
how frugal he had to be to get here?

1748
01:08:58,734 --> 01:09:00,970
He started out with an eel restaurant.

1749
01:09:01,036 --> 01:09:02,171
That's right.

1750
01:09:02,238 --> 01:09:03,973
Do not ever make him mad.

1751
01:09:04,540 --> 01:09:05,808
I'm using the eel.

1752
01:09:05,875 --> 01:09:08,644
-You are? He wants you to.
-Yes.

1753
01:09:08,711 --> 01:09:11,547
-I'll grill them.
-Just grill them. Yes.

1754
01:09:11,614 --> 01:09:12,882
Put that on a skewer too.

1755
01:09:12,948 --> 01:09:14,950
Jung-hwan, five minutes remain.

1756
01:09:15,017 --> 01:09:17,086
It looks like he'll make an eel skewer.

1757
01:09:17,153 --> 01:09:18,454
Mr. Chairman.

1758
01:09:18,521 --> 01:09:20,489
He's Chief Ahn Jung-hwan
of External Affairs.

1759
01:09:20,556 --> 01:09:22,358
He's quite sharp.

1760
01:09:22,424 --> 01:09:25,594
You're just grilling a pre-made product.

1761
01:09:25,661 --> 01:09:26,896
That's right.

1762
01:09:26,962 --> 01:09:28,364
How brazen.

1763
01:09:29,732 --> 01:09:31,066
That's pretty brazen of you.

1764
01:09:31,133 --> 01:09:32,501
He had to point that out.

1765
01:09:32,568 --> 01:09:35,771
We think that soup looks a bit too salty.

1766
01:09:35,838 --> 01:09:38,274
-The soup?
-Yes, try just the soup.

1767
01:09:38,340 --> 01:09:39,909
-The soup.
-Okay.

1768
01:09:39,975 --> 01:09:40,976
Who said it looked salty?

1769
01:09:41,043 --> 01:09:42,011
-Me.
-Him.

1770
01:09:42,077 --> 01:09:44,547
-Him.
-Chef Sung-jun said it looked salty.

1771
01:09:44,613 --> 01:09:46,782
Let's see if it's too salty.

1772
01:09:46,849 --> 01:09:49,685
Chief Ahn of External Affairs.

1773
01:09:49,752 --> 01:09:52,855
Can he call Ho-young a punk?

1774
01:09:52,922 --> 01:09:55,558
Can he call Ho-young a punk?

1775
01:09:55,624 --> 01:09:57,226
I think he'll call him a punk.

1776
01:09:57,293 --> 01:09:59,628
-It's not too salty.
-It's not?

1777
01:09:59,695 --> 01:10:01,597
-But it looked salty.
-And not too sweet?

1778
01:10:01,664 --> 01:10:03,065
-It's perfect.
-It is?

1779
01:10:03,132 --> 01:10:05,401
-But the moment it boils down…
-It'll be salty.

1780
01:10:05,467 --> 01:10:07,069
…he'll get a steamy potato.

1781
01:10:07,136 --> 01:10:08,837
-No.
-It's perfect now.

1782
01:10:08,904 --> 01:10:10,272
-The loser gets a potato?
-Yes.

1783
01:10:10,339 --> 01:10:12,041
-Sounds good.
-He'll have to win.

1784
01:10:12,107 --> 01:10:14,243
Is it spicy?

1785
01:10:14,310 --> 01:10:15,144
No.

1786
01:10:15,444 --> 01:10:16,745
UNSURE

1787
01:10:16,812 --> 01:10:20,316
-Throw in the peppers.
-It's not spicy at all.

1788
01:10:20,382 --> 01:10:22,318
The soup should have a bit of spice.

1789
01:10:22,384 --> 01:10:23,953
Drop more than you think it needs.

1790
01:10:24,186 --> 01:10:26,021
THE MARINATED THIN SKIRT

1791
01:10:26,088 --> 01:10:28,424
He's grilling the meat now.

1792
01:10:28,490 --> 01:10:31,327
-Look at that. That's amazing.
-Thin skirt?

1793
01:10:31,393 --> 01:10:33,729
I'll mix it with cilantro.

1794
01:10:33,796 --> 01:10:36,799
-Later?
-Thin skirt can't taste bad.

1795
01:10:36,865 --> 01:10:39,235
It goes so well with cilantro.

1796
01:10:39,301 --> 01:10:40,336
-Jung-hwan.
-Yes?

1797
01:10:40,402 --> 01:10:43,505
Try Ho-young's <i>champon</i> soup.

1798
01:10:43,572 --> 01:10:46,976
Is the soup good enough
to make Sam stop nagging?

1799
01:10:47,376 --> 01:10:49,044
WILL IT STOP THE NAGGING SAM KIM?

1800
01:10:49,111 --> 01:10:52,081
-He can keep nagging.
-Oh, no.

1801
01:10:52,147 --> 01:10:54,717
-He can keep nagging.
-Oh, no.

1802
01:10:54,783 --> 01:10:56,285
-Emergency.
-Emergency.

1803
01:10:56,352 --> 01:10:57,786
It doesn't taste spicy.

1804
01:10:57,853 --> 01:10:59,588
-That's bad news.
-The important thing is…

1805
01:10:59,655 --> 01:11:01,957
-Throw in some more chili peppers.
-The important thing is,

1806
01:11:02,024 --> 01:11:03,659
the chairman wanted spicy food.

1807
01:11:03,726 --> 01:11:05,127
But neither of them is spicy.

1808
01:11:05,194 --> 01:11:07,196
-Gosh.
-Only two minutes remain.

1809
01:11:07,263 --> 01:11:09,064
Humor the chairman's taste!

1810
01:11:09,131 --> 01:11:11,867
Mr. Chairman wants spicy food!

1811
01:11:11,934 --> 01:11:13,535
I thought <i>champon</i> would be spicier,

1812
01:11:13,602 --> 01:11:15,938
but it didn't live up to the expectation.

1813
01:11:16,005 --> 01:11:17,373
It must be spicier.

1814
01:11:17,439 --> 01:11:21,010
I also love to drink,
and I'd want it to be much spicier.

1815
01:11:21,076 --> 01:11:23,045
Get out if you won't make spicy food!

1816
01:11:23,112 --> 01:11:24,947
Anyway, potatoes are steaming in the back.

1817
01:11:25,014 --> 01:11:26,615
Right.

1818
01:11:26,682 --> 01:11:29,285
When the chairman likes something,
he only goes there.

1819
01:11:30,419 --> 01:11:33,722
He spends a fortune on meals
with his corporate card.

1820
01:11:33,789 --> 01:11:36,492
Which place will he go to?

1821
01:11:36,825 --> 01:11:38,961
MAKING SEA URCHIN ROE NOODLE SAUCE

1822
01:11:39,461 --> 01:11:44,633
2. MIX TSUYU, WATER, SUGAR, PERILLA OIL,
CHOPPED GREEN ONION, CHEONGYANG PEPPER

1823
01:11:45,401 --> 01:11:47,369
He said he'd make cold noodles.

1824
01:11:47,970 --> 01:11:49,605
-Sea urchin roe noodles.
-Wheat noodles.

1825
01:11:49,672 --> 01:11:50,873
NOODLES GO INTO THE SAUCE

1826
01:11:52,675 --> 01:11:54,843
PLATING THE NOODLES RIGHT AWAY

1827
01:11:55,411 --> 01:11:57,112
TOP WITH SEA URCHIN ROE AND FINISH

1828
01:11:57,179 --> 01:11:59,848
Wait a second. The cold noodles.

1829
01:11:59,915 --> 01:12:02,151
This is something
the chairman would be pleased with.

1830
01:12:02,384 --> 01:12:04,119
LOOKS CLASSY ENOUGH FOR HIM

1831
01:12:04,186 --> 01:12:06,588
He makes delicious soy sauce noodles.

1832
01:12:06,655 --> 01:12:08,490
-He's the expert.
-Yes.

1833
01:12:08,557 --> 01:12:10,959
He built his empire with those noodles.

1834
01:12:11,026 --> 01:12:13,295
That's how this business was built.

1835
01:12:13,362 --> 01:12:14,997
The chairman looks worried.

1836
01:12:15,364 --> 01:12:20,469
2. MIX CILANTRO WITH SESAME, VINEGAR,
RED PEPPER POWDER, SOY SAUCE, AND SUGAR

1837
01:12:20,703 --> 01:12:22,471
PREPARING THE SIDE SAUCE

1838
01:12:23,105 --> 01:12:25,407
1 EGG AND 3 SEA URCHIN ROE

1839
01:12:25,808 --> 01:12:26,642
SEASONED SOY SAUCE

1840
01:12:28,877 --> 01:12:31,413
SAUCE FOR THIN SKIRT AND CILANTRO

1841
01:12:31,480 --> 01:12:34,783
-It must look spicy for him to crave soju.
-Add more Cheongyang peppers.

1842
01:12:34,850 --> 01:12:36,251
I did.

1843
01:12:36,318 --> 01:12:37,353
Chop some more in.

1844
01:12:37,419 --> 01:12:38,887
-I added a lot.
-You did?

1845
01:12:38,954 --> 01:12:40,656
The shrimp are plump.

1846
01:12:40,723 --> 01:12:41,990
They are.

1847
01:12:42,057 --> 01:12:45,060
-The shrimp.
-The shrimp he got for free.

1848
01:12:45,127 --> 01:12:47,830
-Forty seconds left.
-Forty seconds.

1849
01:12:47,896 --> 01:12:50,165
-The skewers are done too.
-Time to wrap up.

1850
01:12:50,232 --> 01:12:52,134
-Serve the eel too.
-The eel.

1851
01:12:52,201 --> 01:12:54,269
Those will call for endless soju.

1852
01:12:54,336 --> 01:12:55,270
Thirty seconds.

1853
01:12:55,637 --> 01:12:56,805
PLATING DELICIOUSLY GRILLED THIN SKIRT

1854
01:12:56,872 --> 01:12:58,674
-Please hurry.
-Wrap it up.

1855
01:12:58,741 --> 01:13:00,442
-You don't have much time.
-Hurry.

1856
01:13:01,009 --> 01:13:04,546
-He tops the meat with cilantro.
-Is that an earthen pot?

1857
01:13:04,613 --> 01:13:07,049
-Twenty seconds.
-Twenty seconds.

1858
01:13:07,116 --> 01:13:09,251
What about the noodles?

1859
01:13:09,318 --> 01:13:11,687
Will it be sukiyaki or Nagasaki <i>champon?</i>

1860
01:13:11,754 --> 01:13:13,322
You're almost out of time.

1861
01:13:13,389 --> 01:13:14,857
Chef Ho-young finishes.

1862
01:13:14,923 --> 01:13:16,425
-Ten seconds.
-Nine.

1863
01:13:16,825 --> 01:13:18,060
SPEEDING UP

1864
01:13:18,727 --> 01:13:20,863
LAST-MINUTE CHEONGYANG PEPPERS

1865
01:13:20,929 --> 01:13:21,764
Seven.

1866
01:13:21,830 --> 01:13:22,731
-Six.
-Six.

1867
01:13:22,798 --> 01:13:23,999
-Five.
-Five.

1868
01:13:24,066 --> 01:13:25,000
-Four.
-Four.

1869
01:13:25,067 --> 01:13:26,935
-Three. Two.
-Three. Two.

1870
01:13:27,002 --> 01:13:29,371
-One. It's over.
-One. Sam's done.

1871
01:13:29,872 --> 01:13:31,407
Great job, both of you.

1872
01:13:31,673 --> 01:13:33,475
A HEARTY AMOUNT OF FOOD

1873
01:13:34,176 --> 01:13:36,678
Sam goes in for a handshake.

1874
01:13:37,012 --> 01:13:39,581
WILL THE DISHES MAKE HIM CRAVE SOJU?

1875
01:13:39,648 --> 01:13:41,817
-They look delicious.
-They do.

1876
01:13:41,884 --> 01:13:45,754
Both chefs finished cooking
for Chairman Joo, Chairman Booze.

1877
01:13:45,821 --> 01:13:48,857
The chairman stares at the food.

1878
01:13:48,924 --> 01:13:50,893
-He's in character.
-Yes.

1879
01:13:51,627 --> 01:13:53,896
-Chairman Booze, Time for Some Soju.
-Chairman Booze, Time for Some Soju.

1880
01:13:54,263 --> 01:13:55,097
DEAD SERIOUS

1881
01:13:55,164 --> 01:13:57,966
Do the dishes already make you crave soju?

1882
01:13:58,033 --> 01:14:01,170
Everything looks good.
They'd go well with soju.

1883
01:14:01,236 --> 01:14:02,538
And now,

1884
01:14:02,604 --> 01:14:03,972
serve me some.

1885
01:14:04,306 --> 01:14:07,409
FIRST, HO-YOUNG'S DISH

1886
01:14:08,210 --> 01:14:15,050
RICH AND SPICY NAGASAKI CHAMPON

1887
01:14:16,485 --> 01:14:21,123
SEASONED THIN SKIRT WITH LOTS OF CILANTRO

1888
01:14:22,157 --> 01:14:25,527
CHEF JUNG HO-YOUNG'S
NAGGING SAM KIM CHAMPON

1889
01:14:25,594 --> 01:14:27,563
TAKING HIS TIME

1890
01:14:27,629 --> 01:14:29,398
Choi, do you want his potato?

1891
01:14:29,631 --> 01:14:31,934
PLEASE SPARE MY TEETH

1892
01:14:32,000 --> 01:14:33,702
-Move it.
-You'll get potatoes.

1893
01:14:33,769 --> 01:14:34,603
HE'S SLOW TOO

1894
01:14:34,670 --> 01:14:37,272
What are you waiting for, Manager Poong?

1895
01:14:37,339 --> 01:14:38,173
I'm on it.

1896
01:14:38,240 --> 01:14:40,042
Is his surname Kim or Poong?

1897
01:14:40,108 --> 01:14:42,878
-How did you become a manager?
-Good grief.

1898
01:14:42,945 --> 01:14:44,646
The chairman is starving.

1899
01:14:44,713 --> 01:14:46,114
Move it quickly.

1900
01:14:46,181 --> 01:14:48,317
If anyone gets on your nerves,
we'll send them away.

1901
01:14:48,383 --> 01:14:49,651
Chef Ho-young is here.

1902
01:14:49,718 --> 01:14:51,420
-Move it, Manager Choi.
-"Manager Choi."

1903
01:14:51,487 --> 01:14:52,688
MOVES AS HE'S TOLD

1904
01:14:52,754 --> 01:14:54,156
THE MANAGER HAS LESS POWER

1905
01:14:54,223 --> 01:14:56,291
Assistant Manager Jung, how dare you?

1906
01:14:56,358 --> 01:14:58,126
What are your titles?

1907
01:14:58,193 --> 01:15:00,262
-The hierarchy is a mess.
-Sea urchin roe and egg yolk.

1908
01:15:00,329 --> 01:15:01,330
Let me serve the sauce.

1909
01:15:01,396 --> 01:15:02,898
-Gosh.
-The hierarchy is a mess.

1910
01:15:02,965 --> 01:15:06,068
Sea urchin roe and egg yolk.

1911
01:15:06,134 --> 01:15:07,803
Please mix it well before you eat it.

1912
01:15:08,303 --> 01:15:09,905
AMAZED

1913
01:15:09,972 --> 01:15:11,673
-Enjoy, sir.
-That's amazing, Mr. Jung.

1914
01:15:12,508 --> 01:15:14,142
Assistant manager suits him.

1915
01:15:14,209 --> 01:15:15,811
-Assistant Manager Jung.
-Soju…

1916
01:15:16,478 --> 01:15:17,446
Mr. Jung, soju!

1917
01:15:17,513 --> 01:15:19,414
-Yes, soju!
-Have you lost your mind?

1918
01:15:20,215 --> 01:15:21,517
-What's with you?
-I'm sorry!

1919
01:15:21,583 --> 01:15:22,718
"Have you lost your mind?"

1920
01:15:22,784 --> 01:15:23,852
I've never seen him run.

1921
01:15:24,152 --> 01:15:25,320
MOVED

1922
01:15:25,387 --> 01:15:27,122
What do you think you're doing?

1923
01:15:27,189 --> 01:15:28,390
Mr. Jung.

1924
01:15:28,457 --> 01:15:29,625
Move it.

1925
01:15:29,691 --> 01:15:33,061
-Chief Ahn will serve you.
-He knows what to do.

1926
01:15:33,128 --> 01:15:35,631
Go to the back for a second.

1927
01:15:35,697 --> 01:15:37,299
"Put a potato in your mouth."

1928
01:15:37,366 --> 01:15:38,834
A potato?

1929
01:15:38,901 --> 01:15:41,003
This isn't soju.

1930
01:15:41,069 --> 01:15:42,471
It's my heart, sir.

1931
01:15:43,272 --> 01:15:46,108
Go back to your seat.
What are you waiting for?

1932
01:15:47,910 --> 01:15:50,178
Please stay healthy, sir.

1933
01:15:50,245 --> 01:15:51,813
That's nice.

1934
01:15:51,880 --> 01:15:53,916
GREAT JOB, CHEFS

1935
01:15:53,982 --> 01:15:55,584
He holds soju like it's whiskey.

1936
01:15:55,751 --> 01:15:56,752
CHEERS

1937
01:15:57,386 --> 01:15:59,021
A CASUAL SIP

1938
01:16:00,022 --> 01:16:00,856
GOSH

1939
01:16:01,690 --> 01:16:02,991
It's your usual soju.

1940
01:16:03,191 --> 01:16:04,159
A PLATE, SIR

1941
01:16:05,761 --> 01:16:07,496
BELITTLED

1942
01:16:08,330 --> 01:16:10,632
Your retirement is coming up.

1943
01:16:10,699 --> 01:16:12,768
I GUESS HE'S TRYING TO DO SOMETHING…

1944
01:16:12,834 --> 01:16:15,470
There's nothing much you can do.

1945
01:16:15,537 --> 01:16:17,739
He's the guy who's holding out.

1946
01:16:17,806 --> 01:16:19,641
A bite. He had the meat.

1947
01:16:19,708 --> 01:16:21,410
THE CHAIRMAN SAVORS THE BITE

1948
01:16:21,476 --> 01:16:22,611
Do you enjoy cilantro?

1949
01:16:23,011 --> 01:16:24,546
GLARE

1950
01:16:25,480 --> 01:16:27,015
I'll go outside.

1951
01:16:27,082 --> 01:16:29,351
How dare you? He was eating.

1952
01:16:29,418 --> 01:16:31,587
-He was dining!
-What's with you?

1953
01:16:31,653 --> 01:16:34,790
I'll eat the potato,
get new dentures, and then come back.

1954
01:16:35,157 --> 01:16:36,124
BACK IN HIS CHARACTER

1955
01:16:36,191 --> 01:16:37,559
Come on, Chief Ahn.

1956
01:16:37,626 --> 01:16:39,962
-It's so funny.
-You've seen him eat cilantro.

1957
01:16:40,028 --> 01:16:41,730
It's cilantro.

1958
01:16:42,264 --> 01:16:43,732
Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>

1959
01:16:43,799 --> 01:16:45,367
SLURP

1960
01:16:47,402 --> 01:16:48,904
A SMILE

1961
01:16:49,471 --> 01:16:51,173
-His eyes.
-He approves.

1962
01:16:51,239 --> 01:16:54,076
-Mr. Chairman.
-It's still boiling hard.

1963
01:16:55,744 --> 01:16:57,079
Gosh.

1964
01:16:59,448 --> 01:17:00,282
Goodness.

1965
01:17:00,816 --> 01:17:02,050
SO CURIOUS

1966
01:17:02,117 --> 01:17:04,753
What's the soup like? He's craving soju.

1967
01:17:04,820 --> 01:17:06,154
I see.

1968
01:17:06,922 --> 01:17:07,756
Gosh.

1969
01:17:08,223 --> 01:17:09,558
HAPPY FOR HIM
HE LOOKS SO COOL

1970
01:17:09,625 --> 01:17:11,259
He looks so cool.

1971
01:17:11,326 --> 01:17:14,262
-You can look that cool with soju?
-He makes it look so cool.

1972
01:17:14,630 --> 01:17:16,798
NEXT, CORN NOODLES

1973
01:17:17,132 --> 01:17:18,066
HE SPOTTED SOMETHING?

1974
01:17:18,133 --> 01:17:20,969
If I may interject, sir,

1975
01:17:21,036 --> 01:17:22,270
he forgot the bok choy.

1976
01:17:22,838 --> 01:17:23,705
Shucks.

1977
01:17:24,840 --> 01:17:26,642
He forgot the bok choy.

1978
01:17:26,708 --> 01:17:28,043
Shucks.

1979
01:17:28,810 --> 01:17:30,245
He forgot the bok choy?

1980
01:17:30,312 --> 01:17:34,082
-I noticed your complexion…
-He forgot.

1981
01:17:34,149 --> 01:17:36,585
…and didn't think you should have it.

1982
01:17:36,652 --> 01:17:37,919
What are you talking about?

1983
01:17:38,286 --> 01:17:40,355
SEARCHING

1984
01:17:40,422 --> 01:17:42,290
-He likes bok choy.
-He likes it.

1985
01:17:42,357 --> 01:17:44,826
That's his favorite vegetable.

1986
01:17:44,893 --> 01:17:47,462
I should be eating this with vegetables.

1987
01:17:47,529 --> 01:17:50,298
-He needed vegetables, but…
-He needs some, but…

1988
01:17:50,365 --> 01:17:51,667
Bury him in the field.

1989
01:17:52,000 --> 01:17:54,169
IN THE BOK CHOY FIELD

1990
01:17:54,236 --> 01:17:56,038
He is to be buried!

1991
01:17:56,104 --> 01:17:58,106
Don't you know what the chairman is like?

1992
01:17:58,173 --> 01:17:59,408
MMM!

1993
01:17:59,675 --> 01:18:00,709
DAZED

1994
01:18:02,511 --> 01:18:03,345
THE END

1995
01:18:03,412 --> 01:18:04,746
"Move it."

1996
01:18:04,813 --> 01:18:07,149
He doesn't say much.

1997
01:18:07,516 --> 01:18:09,051
THE NEXT ONE

1998
01:18:09,718 --> 01:18:12,621
HE KINDLY MAKES A GESTURE

1999
01:18:13,021 --> 01:18:14,456
He has no other choice.

2000
01:18:14,523 --> 01:18:16,892
Please do understand, viewers.

2001
01:18:16,958 --> 01:18:21,863
That's his role.
He never really explains anything,

2002
01:18:21,930 --> 01:18:23,532
and he's not too kind.

2003
01:18:23,598 --> 01:18:27,169
-But that makes me move very quickly.
-It does, right?

2004
01:18:27,703 --> 01:18:33,241
SUKIYAKI FULL OF STRIP LOIN
AND LOTS OF VEGGIES

2005
01:18:34,109 --> 01:18:36,645
TRUFFLE EGG YOLK SAUCE

2006
01:18:37,713 --> 01:18:40,982
SEA URCHIN ROE AND SOY SAUCE NOODLES

2007
01:18:42,117 --> 01:18:46,388
CHEF SAM KIM'S JUNG HO-YOUNG! SUKIYAKI!

2008
01:18:46,455 --> 01:18:50,425
General Manager Choi,
who's about to retire very soon.

2009
01:18:50,492 --> 01:18:52,694
-All alone.
-His promotion depends on this.

2010
01:18:52,761 --> 01:18:54,830
Someone named Sam Kim made it.

2011
01:18:54,996 --> 01:18:56,164
GROVEL

2012
01:18:56,631 --> 01:18:57,966
RETREATING…

2013
01:18:58,033 --> 01:18:59,067
It's like a history drama.

2014
01:18:59,134 --> 01:19:00,402
Is this a historical piece?

2015
01:19:00,469 --> 01:19:02,003
-Are you a eunuch?
-A eunuch?

2016
01:19:02,471 --> 01:19:03,939
EUNUCH CHOI, NOT GENERAL MANAGER CHOI

2017
01:19:04,005 --> 01:19:06,708
The eunuch who faces
the most devastating death.

2018
01:19:07,509 --> 01:19:08,777
A devastating one.

2019
01:19:08,844 --> 01:19:11,046
-Sam Kim?
-Sam Kim, sir.

2020
01:19:11,113 --> 01:19:12,814
I added lots of bok choy, sir.

2021
01:19:13,181 --> 01:19:14,783
SAM KIM, YOU…

2022
01:19:14,850 --> 01:19:16,818
I added lots of bok choy, sir.

2023
01:19:16,885 --> 01:19:17,719
SLURP

2024
01:19:20,689 --> 01:19:21,790
HAND ON THE FOREHEAD?

2025
01:19:21,857 --> 01:19:22,858
He has a headache.

2026
01:19:22,924 --> 01:19:24,059
A headache?

2027
01:19:24,126 --> 01:19:24,993
Was it too cold?

2028
01:19:25,894 --> 01:19:27,362
Soju?

2029
01:19:28,296 --> 01:19:31,566
THE SOON-TO-RETIRE MANAGER
IS ALL OVER THE PLACE

2030
01:19:31,633 --> 01:19:33,101
He doesn't want to be fired.

2031
01:19:33,168 --> 01:19:35,637
You could do this?

2032
01:19:36,037 --> 01:19:37,806
HE'S TRYING ALMOST TOO HARD TODAY

2033
01:19:38,440 --> 01:19:39,541
THE NOODLES CALL FOR SOJU

2034
01:19:39,608 --> 01:19:40,942
You're acting like a servant.

2035
01:19:41,009 --> 01:19:44,346
Try to read the chairman's expression.

2036
01:19:44,412 --> 01:19:45,847
Next, the sukiyaki.

2037
01:19:45,914 --> 01:19:49,151
I hope the meat was cooked well.

2038
01:19:49,217 --> 01:19:51,486
-It looks so good.
-It cannot be tough.

2039
01:19:53,889 --> 01:19:55,056
HESITATES

2040
01:19:55,123 --> 01:19:57,292
-It must be tough.
-What is it?

2041
01:19:57,359 --> 01:19:59,661
You should dip it in the egg yolk.

2042
01:20:00,529 --> 01:20:01,997
You should've said so.

2043
01:20:02,197 --> 01:20:04,533
GENERAL MANAGER CHOI FORGOT THE SAUCE

2044
01:20:06,134 --> 01:20:08,170
-General Manager Choi.
-Mr. Choi.

2045
01:20:08,236 --> 01:20:09,404
What have you done?

2046
01:20:09,471 --> 01:20:11,506
Get out of here!

2047
01:20:11,573 --> 01:20:12,874
That's the most important.

2048
01:20:13,441 --> 01:20:15,277
He had the bok choy without the sauce.

2049
01:20:15,944 --> 01:20:18,113
Go and boil a potato for me.

2050
01:20:18,580 --> 01:20:19,815
MOVING QUICKLY

2051
01:20:20,182 --> 01:20:22,150
MANAGER POONG, READ THE ROOM

2052
01:20:22,217 --> 01:20:25,086
-I knew something was missing.
-It's sukiyaki.

2053
01:20:25,554 --> 01:20:27,422
I'M SORRY

2054
01:20:27,823 --> 01:20:29,457
DIPPED INTO THE TRUFFLE EGG YOLK SAUCE

2055
01:20:30,058 --> 01:20:32,427
Manager Poong, you forgot the egg?

2056
01:20:32,494 --> 01:20:34,529
-What do you mean?
-Are you crazy?

2057
01:20:35,597 --> 01:20:36,998
It's probably different, sir.

2058
01:20:37,065 --> 01:20:38,567
-Mmm.
-Yes, sir.

2059
01:20:38,633 --> 01:20:39,801
It looks good.

2060
01:20:40,335 --> 01:20:42,103
SUKIYAKI CALLS FOR SOJU TOO

2061
01:20:42,170 --> 01:20:44,239
Excellent.

2062
01:20:44,306 --> 01:20:47,209
-It's perfect.
-He said one word.

2063
01:20:47,275 --> 01:20:48,877
-Yes.
-Was it spicy?

2064
01:20:48,944 --> 01:20:49,878
CHUGGING SOJU

2065
01:20:51,379 --> 01:20:53,515
VERY PLEASED

2066
01:20:53,748 --> 01:20:54,583
GLANCES

2067
01:20:54,649 --> 01:20:55,851
TRY THE SKEWERS, SIR

2068
01:20:56,051 --> 01:20:57,118
BROKEN
DOES HE WANT SOJU?

2069
01:20:57,786 --> 01:20:59,754
HAS THE DRIVE, BUT ISN'T SO HELPFUL

2070
01:20:59,821 --> 01:21:02,724
It's obvious that
he's never worked a day in an office.

2071
01:21:02,791 --> 01:21:04,726
No, not even for a day.

2072
01:21:04,793 --> 01:21:06,194
I did it for a decade.

2073
01:21:06,261 --> 01:21:07,329
Yet you're like this?

2074
01:21:08,964 --> 01:21:10,532
NEXT, THE CHICKEN

2075
01:21:10,599 --> 01:21:11,900
The chicken.

2076
01:21:12,767 --> 01:21:15,670
Excuse me. I know you're the boss,
but we need a review on this show.

2077
01:21:15,737 --> 01:21:18,406
-I'll do it later, at once.
-At once.

2078
01:21:18,473 --> 01:21:19,875
-He had a plan.
-Chief Ahn.

2079
01:21:19,941 --> 01:21:21,409
I got too ahead.

2080
01:21:21,977 --> 01:21:24,446
-I'm sorry.
-Chief Ahn, why didn't you stay quiet?

2081
01:21:24,512 --> 01:21:26,715
-Good grief.
-I'm sorry.

2082
01:21:26,781 --> 01:21:31,519
Both dishes are great with soju,
and they're absolutely delicious.

2083
01:21:31,586 --> 01:21:33,154
-I'm glad.
-Also,

2084
01:21:33,221 --> 01:21:35,056
this one had

2085
01:21:35,123 --> 01:21:37,759
<i>enough spiciness that I was looking for.</i>

2086
01:21:37,826 --> 01:21:39,794
<i>-Chef Ho-young.</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

2087
01:21:39,861 --> 01:21:43,365
I personally love cilantro,

2088
01:21:43,431 --> 01:21:47,502
and it had a great balance
with thin skirt in this dish.

2089
01:21:47,569 --> 01:21:49,604
A PERFECT MIX OF CILANTRO AND THIN SKIRT

2090
01:21:49,671 --> 01:21:52,941
This one had

2091
01:21:53,008 --> 01:21:57,379
<i>that familiar taste</i>
<i>of perilla oil noodles,</i>

2092
01:21:57,445 --> 01:22:00,282
<i>and sukiyaki also had a familiar taste.</i>

2093
01:22:00,348 --> 01:22:01,249
Another drink.

2094
01:22:02,817 --> 01:22:04,386
He takes yet another drink.

2095
01:22:04,452 --> 01:22:06,588
His glass was empty all this time.

2096
01:22:06,655 --> 01:22:09,791
Can we all try some while he decides?

2097
01:22:09,858 --> 01:22:12,027
First, Chef Ho-young's dish.

2098
01:22:12,093 --> 01:22:13,862
Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>

2099
01:22:13,929 --> 01:22:17,299
You'll find <i>champon</i> and some meat here.

2100
01:22:17,365 --> 01:22:19,901
It's spicy enough.

2101
01:22:19,968 --> 01:22:20,869
It is.

2102
01:22:21,736 --> 01:22:23,672
Good job with the last-minute peppers.

2103
01:22:23,738 --> 01:22:25,440
THIN PORK BELLY AND SHRIMP ROLL

2104
01:22:25,507 --> 01:22:27,809
The thin pork belly and shrimp roll.

2105
01:22:28,410 --> 01:22:29,311
MMM

2106
01:22:29,577 --> 01:22:30,412
SLURP

2107
01:22:33,982 --> 01:22:35,150
Do I not get soju?

2108
01:22:36,017 --> 01:22:38,353
I can't drink
because I'm not the chairman, right?

2109
01:22:38,520 --> 01:22:40,422
PFFT

2110
01:22:40,488 --> 01:22:42,724
Well, you didn't have to cry.

2111
01:22:42,791 --> 01:22:44,659
You can have some
for the sake of evaluation.

2112
01:22:44,726 --> 01:22:46,728
Chief Son, pour her one.

2113
01:22:48,463 --> 01:22:50,632
Cheers to your eyes.
I can't do that to the chairman.

2114
01:22:52,801 --> 01:22:53,635
SPEECHLESS

2115
01:22:53,702 --> 01:22:55,737
I'M SORRY, MR. CHAIRMAN

2116
01:22:55,804 --> 01:22:59,374
I can't say something like that
to the chairman, right?

2117
01:22:59,441 --> 01:23:01,943
-We're at work.
-This is an office.

2118
01:23:02,777 --> 01:23:04,846
Will it go well with soju?

2119
01:23:04,913 --> 01:23:06,081
GOSH♥

2120
01:23:06,514 --> 01:23:08,917
A BITE OF NAGASAKI CHAMPON

2121
01:23:08,984 --> 01:23:11,252
Nagging Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>

2122
01:23:11,319 --> 01:23:12,153
MMM

2123
01:23:12,854 --> 01:23:14,756
What's it like?

2124
01:23:15,991 --> 01:23:17,225
I want to go on a date.

2125
01:23:17,792 --> 01:23:18,626
With whom?

2126
01:23:18,693 --> 01:23:20,295
Not with someone.

2127
01:23:20,362 --> 01:23:23,098
It reminds me of my dates
at a Japanese-style bar.

2128
01:23:23,164 --> 01:23:24,899
Excuse me, don't just say what you want.

2129
01:23:24,966 --> 01:23:29,304
Tell us what the food is like.

2130
01:23:29,371 --> 01:23:30,372
What's that like?

2131
01:23:30,438 --> 01:23:32,474
Just before winter,

2132
01:23:32,540 --> 01:23:35,577
I decided to eat with my date,

2133
01:23:35,643 --> 01:23:37,979
and it felt like that first bite
in that excitement.

2134
01:23:38,046 --> 01:23:40,215
Something that melts away
the day's exhaustion.

2135
01:23:42,117 --> 01:23:46,121
The moment we finish shooting
and step outside,

2136
01:23:46,187 --> 01:23:49,357
it feels like it should be snowing.

2137
01:23:49,924 --> 01:23:53,194
-It must have that warm taste.
-The taste of winter.

2138
01:23:53,261 --> 01:23:56,865
Next, Chef Sam's dish.

2139
01:23:56,931 --> 01:23:58,199
Jung Ho-young! You Sukiyaki!

2140
01:23:58,767 --> 01:24:00,502
SLURP

2141
01:24:00,568 --> 01:24:02,003
There's sea urchin roe.

2142
01:24:03,972 --> 01:24:05,507
My gosh.

2143
01:24:05,573 --> 01:24:08,743
The sea urchin roe
plays a big role for this dish.

2144
01:24:08,810 --> 01:24:13,014
It adds a nice, soft texture,
and it tastes creamy too.

2145
01:24:13,081 --> 01:24:14,749
It's like going to a random restaurant

2146
01:24:14,816 --> 01:24:17,952
while traveling in Japan,
only to find such a nice joint.

2147
01:24:18,019 --> 01:24:19,921
Like, "I didn't think it'd be so good."

2148
01:24:19,988 --> 01:24:22,424
-I had no idea.
-You just walked in.

2149
01:24:22,490 --> 01:24:24,426
Like, "I can't believe
I found this place."

2150
01:24:24,492 --> 01:24:25,693
I should take

2151
01:24:26,561 --> 01:24:28,930
Cheongyang peppers
and add it to sukiyaki in Japan.

2152
01:24:28,997 --> 01:24:32,233
-The spiciness goes well with it.
-It does.

2153
01:24:32,300 --> 01:24:35,770
-It's often very sweet in Japan.
-Yes.

2154
01:24:35,837 --> 01:24:39,441
But Chef Sam cut down on the sweetness,

2155
01:24:39,507 --> 01:24:41,976
so it's easier to eat,
and it goes well with soju.

2156
01:24:42,043 --> 01:24:44,579
If it were sweet,
it wouldn't have been as great.

2157
01:24:44,646 --> 01:24:48,483
Both the grilled eel
and chicken skewer are delicious too.

2158
01:24:48,550 --> 01:24:51,286
-Using the sauce was a smart move.
-Was it?

2159
01:24:51,352 --> 01:24:53,588
-I loved both dishes.
-Yes.

2160
01:24:53,655 --> 01:24:57,058
But which side will Chairman Joo pick?

2161
01:24:57,125 --> 01:24:58,326
<i>Press one for Sam,</i>

2162
01:24:58,993 --> 01:24:59,994
<i>or two for Ho-young.</i>

2163
01:25:01,029 --> 01:25:04,499
Make your decision, and press the button!

2164
01:25:04,999 --> 01:25:06,801
SHAKING LEGS IN CONTEMPLATION

2165
01:25:06,868 --> 01:25:09,604
From the best couple to the worst enemies.

2166
01:25:09,671 --> 01:25:10,872
This was tough.

2167
01:25:10,939 --> 01:25:12,674
It must've been harder
than the previous round.

2168
01:25:12,740 --> 01:25:15,210
-It was.
-That took a moment.

2169
01:25:15,276 --> 01:25:16,945
Both were so good.

2170
01:25:17,011 --> 01:25:19,981
Which chef did he side with?

2171
01:25:20,048 --> 01:25:22,417
Let's find out the result. Show us.

2172
01:25:23,485 --> 01:25:25,286
A perfect dish for soju.

2173
01:25:25,353 --> 01:25:27,255
Ho-young with nine wins,

2174
01:25:27,322 --> 01:25:29,290
and Sam with eight wins.

2175
01:25:29,357 --> 01:25:31,926
Who will turn out to be the winner?

2176
01:25:32,727 --> 01:25:35,730
Which dish did he like better with soju?

2177
01:25:35,797 --> 01:25:38,433
Which dish did he like better with soju?

2178
01:25:38,633 --> 01:25:39,701
JUNG HO-YOUNG WINS

2179
01:25:39,767 --> 01:25:41,369
It was Jung Ho-young.

2180
01:25:41,569 --> 01:25:43,972
CHEF JUNG HO-YOUNG'S
NAGGING SAM KIM CHAMPON WINS

2181
01:25:44,038 --> 01:25:45,673
Jung Ho-young wins.

2182
01:25:45,740 --> 01:25:47,475
HUGGING

2183
01:25:48,042 --> 01:25:51,713
-This was hard.
-You seemed to think for a while.

2184
01:25:51,779 --> 01:25:53,615
It's tough, and there's a lot of pressure.

2185
01:25:53,681 --> 01:25:55,984
So when the result was shown,
I just wanted to run away.

2186
01:25:56,050 --> 01:25:58,686
-Really?
-I don't think I should stay.

2187
01:25:58,753 --> 01:26:01,289
Both dishes were amazing.

2188
01:26:01,356 --> 01:26:03,324
But with soju,

2189
01:26:03,391 --> 01:26:07,295
Sam Kim <i>Champon</i> seemed more suitable.

2190
01:26:07,362 --> 01:26:11,065
-Nagging Sam Kim, not Sam Kim <i>Champon.</i>
-Nagging Sam Kim.

2191
01:26:11,132 --> 01:26:15,370
<i>Nagging Sam Kim </i>Champon
<i>goes better with soju.</i>

2192
01:26:15,436 --> 01:26:19,941
The other dish reminded me
of a really cold glass of beer.

2193
01:26:20,008 --> 01:26:24,078
<i>-It was a delicious sukiyaki.</i>
<i>-The sukiyaki.</i>

2194
01:26:24,145 --> 01:26:27,382
Chef Ho-young takes the victory

2195
01:26:27,448 --> 01:26:31,286
and seizes his tenth win. Congratulations!

2196
01:26:31,352 --> 01:26:33,221
Please put a star on him.

2197
01:26:33,288 --> 01:26:36,858
-He takes his tenth win with <i>champon</i>!
-Thank you.

2198
01:26:36,925 --> 01:26:38,293
-Congratulations.
-Congratulations.

2199
01:26:38,359 --> 01:26:39,861
All right.

2200
01:26:40,828 --> 01:26:43,298
She played Han Yu-ju on <i>Coffee Prince</i>,

2201
01:26:43,364 --> 01:26:45,667
and is now known
as everyone's ex-girlfriend.

2202
01:26:45,733 --> 01:26:47,101
One of her drunken habits

2203
01:26:47,168 --> 01:26:48,136
is headbutting.

2204
01:26:48,203 --> 01:26:49,304
-Headbutting?
-Headbutting?

2205
01:26:49,370 --> 01:26:51,739
If I really like who I'm with,

2206
01:26:51,806 --> 01:26:52,707
I'll headbutt them.

2207
01:26:52,774 --> 01:26:53,775
-Right here?
-Yes.

2208
01:26:53,841 --> 01:26:54,742
I like them so much.

2209
01:26:54,809 --> 01:26:55,810
She's my inspo.

2210
01:26:55,877 --> 01:26:57,645
You look scary, more like a ghost.

2211
01:26:57,712 --> 01:26:58,813
Why do you tilt your head?

2212
01:26:58,880 --> 01:27:00,048
Right? She's like a ghost.

2213
01:27:00,114 --> 01:27:02,350
Gong Yoo suddenly sent me a coffee truck,

2214
01:27:02,417 --> 01:27:04,485
and the banner on the truck said,

2215
01:27:04,552 --> 01:27:05,553
"Banned from twerking."

2216
01:27:05,620 --> 01:27:06,921
You twerk?

2217
01:27:07,121 --> 01:27:08,189
A COOK-OFF FOR JUNG-AN

2218
01:27:08,323 --> 01:27:09,791
BETWEEN GOOD-VIBES-CORE AND LUNACY-CORE

2219
01:27:09,857 --> 01:27:11,125
Eun-yeong seems very calm.

2220
01:27:11,192 --> 01:27:13,695
-A grown woman is calm.
-You seem like one.

2221
01:27:13,895 --> 01:27:14,929
THE GOOD-VIBES GUY IN A HURRY

2222
01:27:15,163 --> 01:27:16,564
OH, NO

2223
01:27:16,731 --> 01:27:17,565
(LOSING HIS MIND) HEH HEH…

2224
01:27:17,632 --> 01:27:19,067
-Emergency.
-I have too much to do.

2225
01:27:19,300 --> 01:27:20,902
STANDING UP, WORRIED

2226
01:27:21,135 --> 01:27:23,171
A REVENGE MATCH TO CURE HIS POONGPHOBIA

2227
01:27:23,404 --> 01:27:24,572
I WILL WIN AGAINST HIM TODAY!

2228
01:27:24,772 --> 01:27:26,040
NAM-NO CAME PREPARED TO WIN

2229
01:27:26,274 --> 01:27:27,709
BUT HE ATTACKS WITH THE DARK CHOPPING(?)

2230
01:27:27,842 --> 01:27:28,676
I'LL WIN TODAY!

2231
01:27:28,876 --> 01:27:31,813
(STEALING POONG'S STUFF)
DOING ANYTHING TO WIN

2232
01:27:32,013 --> 01:27:33,681
CAN HE OVERCOME POONG'S DARK(?) ATTACKS

2233
01:27:33,848 --> 01:27:35,650
AND MAKE A DISH JUNG-AN WILL LOVE?

2234
01:27:39,120 --> 01:27:40,955
She's putting the umbrella behind her ear.

2235
01:27:52,967 --> 01:27:57,739
Subtitle translation by: Eunsook Youn
